ge is opaque; if they arrived here through losses, momentum may be negative. BIG's actual seeding and recent trajectory unknown. TOURNAMENT TIER DISCREPANCY: Search results conflate VCL (regional league) with EMEA Masters (international A-Tier). Misa's recent wins (April 6 vs EF.PA, April 4 vs SCA) appear to be in VCL T\u00fcrkiye, a lower-tier competition. Their standing in the headline EMEA Masters event is unestablished. MURPHY'S LAW ON FAVORITES: Market prices Misa at 58% despite having no meaningful LoL performance data provided. Overconfidence in an unknown favorite often precedes upset. The 16% gap (58-42) is substantial for an all-or-nothing BO3 with zero head-to-head history. BO3 FORMAT VARIANCE: Best-of-three matches have inherent volatility. Even a moderately-matched underdog has ~35-45% win probability in BO3 due to momentum swings, game-specific draft adaptation, and mental factors. A 42% market price for BIG may be underselling BO3 inherent closeness. TIME-TO-REVERSAL CONSTRAINT: Event is TODAY (~5 hours to resolution). Any narrative reversal requires near-immediate shock (roster changes, forfeiture, etc.). However, this same tight timeline means market prices may be stale if trading volume ($54.8K) has dried up in final hours. Late bets on BIG could reflect last-minute intelligence not reflected in 58% YES opening. RESOLUTION CRITERIA AMBIGUITY: Match was 'initially scheduled for April 7 at 2:00PM ET' per briefing timestamp of April 7 19:20 UTC. This suggests match may already be LIVE or concluded, yet briefing does not confirm a result. If match is in-progress or just ended, real-time information favors underdogs whose performance is being revealed in real-time. ADVOCATE POSITION: As underdog advocate, I flag that BIG is priced at 42% despite being a complete unknown \u2014 a tactical advantage in information-sparse markets. The 58% YES price lacks evidentiary support from the briefing. NO deserves higher pricing given symmetric ignorance about both 