lt (2-0 vs Division One) is a clean dominant victory; Alliance Guardians' 2-1 vs Nightblood Gaming required a tiebreaker map, suggesting tighter matches and less dominant playstyle. RANKING SEPARATION: FlyQuest ranked 4th vs Alliance Guardians ranked 5th in current standings despite identical 2-1 records \u2014 the differential acts as the tiebreaker, reinforcing that the market's 74% for FlyQuest is justified by objective performance metrics. UNDERDOG'S WEAKNESSES: Alliance Guardians' high absolute round count (87 total) paired with low differential (+2) indicates they play many close, contested maps \u2014 this is a sign of inconsistency and vulnerability in high-pressure BO3 scenarios where clean execution matters. NO HISTORY CUTS BOTH WAYS: Lack of head-to-head data removes any predictive upset narratives; in such cases, tournament standing (4th vs 5th) and efficiency metrics (differential) become the decisive proxies, both favoring FlyQuest. PLAYOFF IMPLICATIONS: Both teams are 2-1 and chasing the 4-win threshold; this is high-stakes play where the more disciplined, efficient team (FlyQuest) typically prevails over the grittier but less consistent competitor (Alliance Guardians). MARKET PRICING SANITY CHECK: 74% for the 4th-ranked team with better differential and recent dominant form is reasonable, not overheated \u2014 the 26% for Alliance Guardians reflects genuine upset potential but underestimates FlyQuest's structural advantages. CONFIDENCE ASYMMETRY: High confidence in YES (78) because round differential is a robust metric and recent form aligns; moderate confidence in NO (62) because Alliance Guardians' BO3 consistency is unclear \u2014 they could execute cleaner maps, but the briefing offers no evidence of edge cases favoring a collapse or major upset.", "key_factor": "FlyQuest's superior +9 round differential versus Alliance Guardians' +2 indicates more consistent map control and utility execution, the fundamental skill gap that matters most in BO3 co