rable to late-game information or lineup changes. Crowd may be anchoring to initial odds rather than reassessing. Briefing mentions roster lineups but provides zero recent win rates, map pools, or recent tournament placings that would justify 48-point YES premium. Swiss format context noted but not leveraged with actual performance tiers or seeding data \u2014 suggests market is pricing sentiment rather than structure. Rational apathy detected: market is pricing a slight YES lean without sufficient evidentiary support. NO side offers mild contrarian value if teams are truly balanced.", "key_factor": "Apathy", "probability_yes": 0.72, "probability_no": 0.28, "probability_source": "synthetic_mirror"}, {"agent_name": "The Domain Specialist", "probability": 0.62, "confidence_yes": 0.45, "confidence_no": 0.38, "rationale": "Market price (74% YES) suggests strong consensus favoring insane players, but briefing provides no supporting statistics: no recent match results, win rates, map pool strengths, or head-to-head records. Roster composition analysis: insane players (jsfeltner, ayaneuu, TyRa, aelor, Scorchyy) vs Aether (brett, Andrew, Seb, xaler, H0NeST)\u2014both appear to be mid-tier NA players; H0NeST's Ukraine origin provides slight international experience credential for Aether, but this is a minor factor in NA regional circuit. BO3 format reduces single-map upsets but requires sustained strategic consistency across 2-3 maps; briefing shows no map pool analysis, veto tendencies, or recent performance on specific maps for either team. Event timing (TODAY, ~7 hours to match start) and market volume ($8,101 USD) indicate active trading but not deep liquidity; sharp bettors may have information asymmetry unavailable in public briefing. CCT North America Series #4 is a third-tier regional circuit (below T1 Valorant Champs tier); roster stability does not correlate strongly with tournament performance at this level. Briefing includes noise (NRG reference, forum comments about 'savage kid', CYBERSHOKE Prospects mention) with no clear relevance to this specific matchup\u2014suggests data scraping artifact. No recent form data: cannot assess momentum, scrim results, roster changes, or player absences in past 2 weeks. Market price of 74% YES reflects betting-desk confidence, not analytical depth; this may represent informed betting or momentum-driven overpricing. Confidence in YES is MODERATE (45) due to market consensus but lack of corroborating evidence; confidence in NO is SLIGHTLY LOWER (38) because brief mentions Aether's international experience and roster stability without refutation. Probability score of 62 represents cautious agreement with market direction (insane players favored) while heavily discounting for data scarcity and assumption that market-implied 74% may be overextended given total information vacuum.", "key_factor": "The briefing contains fragmented roster data and zero head-to-head history, match statistics, or current form indicators\u2014making this a near-blind evaluation 7 hours before a live BO3 match with high market volatility risk.", "probability_yes": 0.62, "probability_no": 0.38, "probability_source": "synthetic_mirror"}, {"agent_name": "The Contrarian", "probability": 0.68, "confidence_yes": 0.62, "confidence_no": 0.45, "rationale": "Market is trading at 74% YES, but confidence data strongly suggests undervaluation of NO-side risk due to severe information asymmetry \u2014 briefing provides zero recent form data, head-to-head records, or tournament standings for either team. Briefing explicitly states 'lack specific recent performance metrics' and 'no clear indication' of insane players' form \u2014 this is a RED FLAG that council may be anchoring on price rather than evidence. Team Aether's H0NeST is listed as Ukraine-based; online CCT matches have known latency variance for international players, especially during off-peak NA hours (match is 8:30 PM ET). Single-player connection instability in BO3 format is a credible high-impact swing factor. insane players roster is purely North American (US/Canada), eliminating timezone/ISP friction \u2014 structural home-field advantage in online-only format that briefing does not explicitly address. BO3 format means one badly-played map or one player tilt can cascade; if H0NeST has early-series connectivity hiccup, morale and anti-economy decision-making may suffer disproportionately on map 2 or 3. Briefing mentions 'Outfit 49 forfeit the 2nd map' in prior results, showing map-level forfeit/withdrawal precedent in this tournament \u2014 suggests fragile team cohesion or external disruption is historically plausible in this series. NO confidence capped at 45 because briefing provides ZERO statistical counterargument to the 74% YES price \u2014 no recent Aether wins, no form trajectory, no map pool strength data. Absence of counter-evidence is not evidence of weakness. Reduce YES confidence to 62 because 74% market price assumes full information (recent LAN results, head-to-head, ranked ladder position) that briefing explicitly lacks \u2014 price may be inflated by default heuristic rather than edge.", "key_factor": "Team Aether's international player H0NeST experiences connection/latency issues on the online platform, forcing a tactical substitution or forcing Aether into a suboptimal map veto strategy mid-series.", "probability_yes": 0.68, "probability_no": 0.31999999999999995, "probability_source": "synthetic_mirror"}], "final_score": 0.6354920634920636, "no_score": 0.3832958801498127, "edge": -0.10950793650793644, "no_edge": 0.1282958801498127, "recommendation": "PASS", "confidence_level": "MEDIUM", "signal_type": null, "pass_reason": "YES-side confidence too low: 45% (need \u226567%)", "expected_roi": 0.0, "briefing": {"market": {"id": "1876651", "question": "Counter-Strike: insane players vs Team Aether (BO3) - CCT North America Series #4 Group B", "resolution_criteria": "This market refers to the Counter-Strike match 