n and 3.38 KDA are league-leading mid-lane metrics; this level of dominance in single-game high-pressure contexts historically outweighs aggregate team records in LCK playoffs-style matches. The briefing provides NO direct head-to-head record between T1 and Gen.G in 2026; historical pedigree and matchup psychology strongly favor T1 in a single Game 1 snapshot. Gen.G's Canyon (6.00 KDA) and Chovy (4.64 KDA) are genuinely strong, but Oner (50% win rate) has shown resilience; the briefing shows no evidence of a jungle gap that would decisively favor Gen.G in Game 1. Market currently prices YES at 36% (underdog), reflecting consensus belief in Gen.G superiority; this is a contrarian signal that early-season aggregate stats are overweighting recency bias. T1's loss to KT does not reveal whether T1 has addressed weaknesses or whether KT exploited a one-off tactical mismatch; Gen.G has NOT played KT and may face different preparation. Early-season LCK play is volatile; single-game outcomes are often determined by draft phase and early jungle tracking\u2014both T1 strengths historically\u2014rather than aggregate statistics. Keria's 1.1 KDA support stat is anomalously low; if this reflects a recent illness, visa delay, or internal swap unmentioned in briefing, T1's bot lane stabilization in Game 1 could unlock decisive advantages. No mention of coaching changes, meta shifts (e.g., new patch notes between April 1 and April 8), or substitution roster moves; absence of this data in a fast-moving competitive environment is a RED FLAG suggesting briefing is outdated.", "key_factor": "T1 deploys an unexpected roster substitution or off-meta draft strategy that Gen.G's recent film study has not prepared for, combined with Faker micro-shotcalling exploitation of a specific Gen.G jungler weakness in early skirmishes.", "probability_yes": 0.32, "probability_no": 0.6799999999999999, "probability_source": "synthetic_mirror"}], "final_score": 0.43078313253012046, "no_score": 0.569792284