ence in insane players. CCT Series 4 Group B structure with 12 teams and $10K prize pool suggests competitive parity typical of mid-tier North American tournaments \u2014 not a tier-1 vs tier-2 mismatch. Confidence on both sides suppressed due to complete absence of differentiating data: cannot confidently assess why market favors insane players nor why shimmer might upset.", "key_factor": "Apathy", "probability_yes": 0.12, "probability_no": 0.88, "probability_source": "synthetic_mirror"}, {"agent_name": "The Domain Specialist", "probability": 0.15, "confidence_yes": 0.25, "confidence_no": 0.28, "rationale": "CRITICAL DATA GAP: No recent match statistics, win rates, map pool performance, or roster stability data for either shimmer or insane players\u2014standard HLTV/Liquipedia records are absent from briefing. MARKET PRICE AS SIGNAL: The 12% YES / 88% NO split is extreme but not grounded in documented team performance; this appears to reflect speculation or community perception rather than statistical analysis of comparable tier-2 CCT teams. TOURNAMENT CONTEXT: CCT North America Series 4 is a $10k regional qualifier\u2014participants are typically semi-pro or aspirational tier-2 teams with inconsistent rosters and high match volatility; outcome predictability is inherently low for such competitions. BO3 FORMAT RISK: Best-of-3 in low-tier competitive CS is highly sensitive to map vetoes, individual player form on the day, and tactical preparation; without roster/recent form data, directional confidence cannot justify >40% on either side. TEAM IDENTIFICATION AMBIGUITY: 'shimmer' and 'insane players' are generic names with no distinctive roster or sponsorship data provided; no verification that these are stable, documented teams on HLTV or Liquipedia. TIME-TO-EVENT DECAY: Market resolves in ~8 hours; the extremely low YES price (12%) suggests market has made a judgment, but given absence of supporting evidence, this may reflect narrative/bias rather than predictive ac