lects total absence of counter-evidence about LFO strengths.", "key_factor": "Market pricing at 84% YES appears overconfident given complete absence of head-to-head competitive history, recent roster changes in both teams, and an early-season Kickoff tournament where form is volatile.", "probability_yes": 0.72, "probability_no": 0.28, "probability_source": "synthetic_mirror"}, {"agent_name": "The Contrarian", "probability": 0.28, "confidence_yes": 0.35, "confidence_no": 0.42, "rationale": "LFO No Name's recent form is conspicuously absent from briefing: CAG defeated LFO-NN 2:0 on March 27, suggesting recent losses or internal issues not detailed here. Can You Be My Enemy shows unstable but combative recent history: 0-2 to Dplus (April 3), but 2-1 victories over VITE and Team Function Kyoto in Challenger Series (March 13-15), indicating capacity for upset when not facing top seeds. Hinatun listed as Can You Be My Enemy substitute on 2026-03-06; if activated for this lower-bracket match, introduces unknown substitution risk factor for LFO No Name's preparation\u2014substitutes often enable tactical surprise or serve as form reset. Hamamoto appointed Can You Be My Enemy coach on 2026-03-06; coaching/tactical innovation is plausible edge at lower-bracket stage where LFO may lack rigorous preparation, especially post-loss. Briefing provides zero direct head-to-head statistics or recent LFO No Name results, only aggregate regional data and Can You Be My Enemy Kickoff performance (0-2 to Dplus); market may be pricing in outdated seed expectations rather than current form. Map veto asymmetry invisible: Can You Be My Enemy's historic picks (Lair vs Clubhouse vs Bank bans) differ from broader meta, suggesting nonconformist draft strategy that could ambush rigid preparation. Border (75% defender win rate) and Clubhouse (62.1%) are high-variance defender-favored maps; if Can You Be My Enemy wins veto and forces one of these, defensive discipline could flip series outcome. 84% Y