{"run_id": "b74f3f5b-749d-4b54-808a-67472c0ec2af", "timestamp": "2026-04-10T12:10:46.501089+00:00", "legacy": false, "result": {"market": {"id": "1933615", "question": "LoL: EKO Esports vs Aeterna Esports (BO1) - LIT Regular Season", "resolution_criteria": "This market refers to the LoL match between EKO Esports and Aeterna Esports in the LIT Regular Season, initially scheduled for April 10 at 12:00PM ET.\n\nThis market will resolve to \"EKO Esports\" if EKO Esports win the match against Aeterna Esports.\n\nThis market will resolve to \"Aeterna Esports\" if Aeterna Esports win the match against EKO Esports.\n\nIf the match is canceled (not played at all), ends in a tie, or is delayed beyond 7 days from the scheduled date without a winner determined, this market will resolve to 50-50. \n\nIf the match begins but is not completed, and one team wins due to the opponent's forfeiture, disqualification, or walkover, this market will resolve to the team who wins. \n\nIf the match ends in a forfeit, disqualification, or walkover (team withdraws before the start and the other wins automatically), this market will resolve to 50-50. \n\nThe resolution source for this market will be official information from https://gol.gg/esports/home. However, if https://gol.gg/esports/home has not published final results within 2 hours after the event\u2019s conclusion, a consensus of credible reporting may be used instead including video evidence.\n\nIn cases where a team\u2019s listed name includes minor discrepancies from the resolution source, this market will resolve based on the underlying real-world match rather than exact name matching. Recognizable abbreviations, alternate or erroneous spellings, sponsor tags, affiliate or academy designations, regional identifiers, and minor formatting differences will be treated as referring to the same team, provided the intended team can be clearly and uniquely identified within the relevant competition. If a listed team name has no reasonable connection to any participating team, or if it matches or could reasonably refer to another team in the same competition such that the intended team cannot be unambiguously determined, this market will resolve 50-50.", "end_date": "2026-04-10T22:00:00Z", "event_start_time": "2026-04-10T16:00:00Z", "yes_price": 0.705, "volume_usd": 790.5421729999999, "category": "league-of-legends"}, "verdicts": [{"agent_name": "The Rules Lawyer", "probability": 0.7, "confidence_yes": 0.35, "confidence_no": 0.3, "rationale": "OUTCOME CONDITION: EKO Esports must win the BO1 match scheduled for April 10, 12:00 PM ET in LIT Regular Season. RESOLUTION MECHANISM: Clear criteria for normal completion (winner declared), forfeits (50-50), and cancellations (50-50). No ambiguity in criteria text. CRITICAL DATA GAP: Briefing contains zero analytical data on either team\u2014no win rates, roster strength, recent form, standings position, head-to-head history, or seasonal performance metrics. Aeterna Esports is confirmed active (match vs HMBLE on April 3, 2026), but EKO Esports has no retrievable performance data in briefing. Market price of 70% YES reflects trader consensus, but briefing explicitly notes 'insufficient public data on both teams' recent form and roster strength.' BO1 format inherently high variance; single game outcome poorly predictable without team strength differential data. No seeding, standings, or playoff context provided; cannot assess relative tier positioning or momentum. CONFIDENCE ASYMMETRY JUSTIFIED: High confidence_yes (35) and confidence_no (30) both LOW because neither side has evidentiary foundation. Briefing provides confirmation only that Aeterna exists as active team; EKO existence unconfirmed in data. Probability_score of 70 is anchored to market price, not underlying analytics. This reflects rational uncertainty exploitation by traders rather than analytical edge. EVENT TIMING: Match occurs TODAY (~10 hours from briefing timestamp). No time for reversal from data discovery; outcome will resolve on actual match result. RESOLUTION RISK: gol.gg is specified primary source; backup credible reporting available. Low oracle risk given LIT is official league with public broadcast infrastructure.", "key_factor": "Critical data gap: briefing provides no team performance metrics, head-to-head history, current standings, or form data for either EKO or Aeterna Esports; probability estimate reflects market price alignment rather than analytical foundation.", "probability_yes": 0.7, "probability_no": 0.30000000000000004, "probability_source": "synthetic_mirror"}, {"agent_name": "The Bull", "probability": 0.65, "confidence_yes": 0.42, "confidence_no": 0.38, "rationale": "FAVORED SIDE ANALYSIS (YES = EKO wins): Market has priced EKO at 70%, a 7:3 advantage over Aeterna despite publicly available data being extremely sparse. The briefing explicitly states 'limited information' and 'no direct head-to-head data, recent form metrics, or specific performance information' \u2014 this information vacuum suggests the 70% YES price is driven by non-public intelligence (scout reports, internal team rankings, roster knowledge among professional esports bettors). EKO's 70% pricing in a two-team binary match indicates market participants have confidence in EKO's strength relative to Aeterna, even without published justification in this briefing. UNDERDOG SIDE WEAKNESSES (NO = Aeterna wins): Aeterna's only confirmed data point is a match vs HMBLE on April 3, 2026 \u2014 we do not know if Aeterna won or lost that match, making recent form assessment impossible. 30% underdog pricing for Aeterna suggests market consensus views them as materially weaker than EKO, despite both teams lacking public performance transparency. BO1 format increases variance but does not eliminate team strength disparity \u2014 even in single games, the stronger team typically prevails more often. Event is TODAY (10 hours to resolution) \u2014 insufficient time for major roster changes, injuries, or other reversals to substantially alter the underlying probability; market snapshot reflects current team states. CONFIDENCE LIMITATION: Both confidence scores are moderate (42/38) because the briefing provides NO quantitative form data, win rates, head-to-head records, or published standings. The 70% YES price is likely based on information NOT in this briefing (professional scouting, community consensus, or team-specific datasets). Setting probability at 65 (below the 70% market price) reflects slight skepticism that the market may be overweighting EKO in the absence of transparent justification, but respects the directional consensus that EKO is stronger. The underdog NO case cannot be strengthened without data; Aeterna's low price may be deserved or may reflect insufficient market attention to a capable dark horse.", "key_factor": "Market pricing of YES at 70% reflects aggregated betting intelligence on team strength absent from public briefing data.", "probability_yes": 0.65, "probability_no": 0.35, "probability_source": "synthetic_mirror"}, {"agent_name": "The Bear", "probability": 0.35, "confidence_yes": 0.28, "confidence_no": 0.26, "rationale": "UNDERDOG CASE (NO \u2014 Aeterna Esports): The briefing reveals a critical data vacuum. NO performance metrics exist for EKO Esports in the current season, yet the market prices them at 70% favorites. This is sentiment-driven pricing without substantive support. UNDERDOG STRENGTH \u2014 Information Asymmetry: Aeterna Esports is confirmed active and recently competed (April 3 vs HMBLE), providing at least one recent match reference point. EKO Esports has zero documented recent activity in the briefing, suggesting possible roster changes, preparation gaps, or lower tier standing. UNDERDOG STRENGTH \u2014 BO1 Volatility: Single-game formats eliminate skill consistency as a predictor. Upsets occur at substantially higher rates in BO1 than BO3/BO5. Without head-to-head history or form data, the 70% confidence in EKO is unjustifiable in this format. FAVORITE VULNERABILITY: EKO Esports is effectively an unknown entity in this briefing. The 70% price reflects either market assumption of seed/seeding hierarchy (not provided) or pure name recognition \u2014 neither is evidenced. No roster, recent results, or tournament standing data supports the heavy favorite designation. FAVORITE VULNERABILITY: The briefing explicitly states 'no head-to-head history or matchup analysis available' and 'insufficient public data on both teams' recent form.' This should suppress confidence in either direction, but the market heavily skews YES regardless. AETERNA RECENT ACTIVITY: Aeterna's April 3 match (vs HMBLE) provides a concrete anchor point. If that match outcome is retrievable, it signals team momentum, roster stability, and current competitive level \u2014 EKO has no equivalent anchor. CONFIDENCE ASYMMETRY: YES confidence is low (28%) because the briefing provides zero performance justification for the 70% price. NO confidence is equally low (26%) because Aeterna's single match reference is insufficient to build a strong upset case. This is a data-starved market. REALISTIC UPSET PATHWAY: Aeterna wins if EKO is: (a) a lower-tier or academy team elevated in market perception, (b) experiencing roster instability, or (c) under-prepared for the match. The briefing rules out data to confirm or refute any of these \u2014 ambiguity favors the underdog in BO1. MARKET MISPRICE INDICATOR: A 70/30 split on a BO1 with zero documented head-to-head history and absent EKO performance data is inconsistent with rational expected value. The underdog is likely underpriced. RESOLUTION TIMING: Match occurs TODAY in ~10 hours. No time for material news to shift the market. Current prices reflect prior assumption, not late-breaking information. This increases the role of hidden factors favoring Aeterna. CONCLUSION: Probability_score of 35 reflects that while EKO may deserve slight favorite status, the 70% market price is unjustified by briefing evidence. Aeterna Esports has better relative documentation (one confirmed match) and BO1 format favors upset risk. NO is underpriced.", "key_factor": "Complete absence of performance data on EKO Esports creates asymmetric information favoring the underdog, and BO1 format eliminates consistency as a predictive factor.", "probability_yes": 0.35, "probability_no": 0.65, "probability_source": "synthetic_mirror"}, {"agent_name": "The Base-Rate Statistician", "probability": 0.5, "confidence_yes": 0.25, "confidence_no": 0.25, "rationale": "CRITICAL DATA GAP: Briefing contains no team-level performance data, head-to-head history, recent form metrics, roster composition, or LIT 2026 Spring standings for either EKO Esports or Aeterna Esports. MARKET PRICING SIGNAL: Current market price of 70% YES suggests informed traders believe EKO Esports is materially stronger, but this signal cannot be validated against the briefing document's available facts. BO1 FORMAT: Best-of-1 matches inherently have high variance; even a moderately favored team (e.g., 60% true win rate) can lose with meaningful probability due to single-game volatility. AETERNA CONFIRMATION: Only confirmed fact is Aeterna Esports' participation in LIT 2026 Spring (match vs HMBLE on April 3). No equivalent confirmation or performance data exists for EKO Esports in the briefing. ASYMMETRIC EVIDENCE: One team is confirmed as active; the other (EKO) has zero mentions in briefing. This mild asymmetry does not justify moving significantly away from 50-50 without performance data. BASE RATE LOGIC: Without roster strength, recent win rates, or head-to-head metrics, regression to 50% is appropriate. Market may be reflecting real information (team rankings, standings) not captured in the briefing. TIME PRESSURE: Match occurs TODAY (~10 hours from briefing timestamp). A significant reversal requiring hidden team performance data is unlikely to emerge in public sources before resolution, but shares will trade near $0.01/$0.99 once outcome becomes clear. CONFIDENCE SYMMETRY: Both confidence scores set to 25 because the briefing provides near-zero actionable discrimination between the teams. High confidence in either YES or NO would be unjustified overconfidence.", "key_factor": "In professional League of Legends best-of-1 matches with no available team performance data, outcome probability approaches 50-50; team strength variance in regional leagues is high but unknowable without roster/form metrics.", "probability_yes": 0.5, "probability_no": 0.5, "probability_source": "synthetic_mirror"}, {"agent_name": "The Sentiment Analyst", "probability": 0.58, "confidence_yes": 0.28, "confidence_no": 0.26, "rationale": "Market is pricing YES (EKO) at 70% despite zero performance data on EKO Esports \u2014 this reflects crowd speculation rather than informed analysis. Briefing confirms Aeterna Esports is active (match vs HMBLE on Apr 3), but provides NO recent form, standings position, or head-to-head history for either team. 70-30 split suggests the market has developed a directional lean, but the reasoning is opaque \u2014 likely based on name recognition, seed position (unknown), or Reddit/community sentiment not reflected in briefing. BO1 format inherently reduces predictability; single-game elimination amplifies variance and makes crowd consensus even less reliable. Briefing explicitly states 'Limited information is available' and 'no direct head-to-head data, recent form metrics, or specific performance information' \u2014 market is pricing conviction on a data vacuum. Both teams lack sufficient counter-evidence to justify high confidence in either direction; this is a true uncertainty event being treated as a known outcome. Time-to-resolution is ~10 hours (TODAY) \u2014 no time for new information to move the needle; current price likely reflects pre-match speculation and betting sentiment, not fundamental analysis. Given symmetric information deficit, true probability should be near 50-50; the 70-30 skew suggests irrational crowding toward YES, but insufficient briefing data to identify which side is truly mispriced. Confidence scores must reflect data poverty: both confidence_yes and confidence_no remain low because neither side can be defensibly estimated from available evidence.", "key_factor": "Apathy", "probability_yes": 0.58, "probability_no": 0.42000000000000004, "probability_source": "synthetic_mirror"}, {"agent_name": "The Domain Specialist", "probability": 0.68, "confidence_yes": 0.28, "confidence_no": 0.24, "rationale": "CRITICAL DATA GAP: Briefing contains zero performance metrics, win-loss records, roster compositions, patch adaptation data, or head-to-head history for either EKO Esports or Aeterna Esports in LIT 2026 Spring. MARKET PRICE ANALYSIS: YES side priced at 70% suggests market participants hold directional bias toward EKO, but low trading volume ($791 USD) indicates thin liquidity and potential vulnerability to informed contrarian bets. AETERNA CONFIRMATION ONLY: Briefing confirms Aeterna Esports participated in LIT 2026 Spring (match vs HMBLE on April 3, 2026); EKO Esports has zero confirmation of participation or roster activity. BO1 FORMAT RISK: Best-of-1 matches in competitive LoL are inherently high-variance; single-game outcomes depend heavily on draft, early jungle proximity, and macro coordination \u2014 data-scarce matchups have elevated upset potential. ESPORTS-SPECIFIC UNCERTAINTY: League of Legends patch cycles, roster changes mid-split, and regional competitive dynamics (LIT spans Iberian Peninsula teams) are not addressed in briefing; 2026 Spring meta and team adaptability remain unknown. TIMING PRESSURE: EVENT TIMING = TODAY with ~10-hour resolution window; any reversal (e.g., last-minute roster change, game cancellation) has minimal room for discovery before payout; this reduces confidence in both directions. SYMMETRICAL IGNORANCE PROBLEM: Absence of data favors neither side; confidence in YES (EKO win) and confidence in NO (Aeterna win) are both legitimately weak due to identical information asymmetry. MARKET PRICE AS PRIOR: The 70% YES price may reflect informed market-maker positioning based on unpublished team strength assessments, but without corroborating briefing data, treating this as ground truth introduces circular reasoning.", "key_factor": "In BO1 esports matches with no roster/form data available, the market price reflects primarily liquidity sentiment rather than informed odds; both teams' actual competitive standing remains opaque with ~10 hours until match execution.", "probability_yes": 0.68, "probability_no": 0.31999999999999995, "probability_source": "synthetic_mirror"}, {"agent_name": "The Contrarian", "probability": 0.55, "confidence_yes": 0.28, "confidence_no": 0.25, "rationale": "CRITICAL DATA GAP: The briefing provides zero performance metrics, head-to-head history, recent form trajectories, or roster compositions for either EKO or Aeterna. The 70-30 market pricing suggests strong confidence in EKO, but no supporting evidence is present in the briefing. BLACK SWAN \u2014 ROSTER VOLATILITY: BO1 formats are uniquely vulnerable to personnel shocks. A single player substitution (e.g., EKO's jungler benched mid-series due to illness, forcing an academy-level replacement) can swing a 70-30 matchup to 40-60 in hours. This is competitively plausible and almost never captured in pre-match market data, especially in lower-profile regional leagues. ABSENCE OF SIGNAL = ABSENCE OF CONFIDENCE: Aeterna was confirmed active on April 3 vs HMBLE, but no EKO match record appears in briefing. This absence could indicate: (a) EKO is lower-tier and has fewer publicized matches, (b) EKO is a newer roster with volatile performance, or (c) EKO data simply wasn't indexed. Any of these scenarios weakens the 70% certainty. BO1 FORMAT AMPLIFIES VARIANCE: In a single-game format, a single macro mistake, draft miss-click, or early jungle gank swing kills the entire match. The market is pricing EKO at 70%, but in BO1s, the intrinsic variance is ~15-20% higher than BO3 equivalents. Current pricing may be borrowing confidence from team brand/history without acknowledging format risk. TIMING & RECENCY BLINDNESS: The match is TODAY (~10 hours to resolution). If a roster substitution is announced in the next 4-6 hours (e.g., on team Twitter/Discord), Polymarket prices will not update instantly. Early movers who catch this signal before market repricing will exploit the 70-30 mispricing catastrophically. PSYCHOLOGICAL EDGE FOR UNDERDOG: Aeterna may be mentally fresher if EKO is favored. In BO1 knockout scenarios (which this appears to mimic), favorite teams often play passive/conservative to 'not lose' while underdogs play aggressive and win early. No motivation/pressure data is available. SPLIT CONFIDENCE REFLECTS DATA VACUUM: confidence_yes and confidence_no are both low (28 and 25) because the briefing provides almost no grounds to defend either team. The 55 probability score is near-neutral, reflecting genuine uncertainty masked by a market price that appears overconfident in EKO.", "key_factor": "Roster substitution or last-minute lineup change (player illness, emergency stand-in) for either team not yet reflected in public records, drastically altering mid-lane or support dynamics in a BO1 format where a single weak link can cascade into defeat.", "probability_yes": 0.55, "probability_no": 0.44999999999999996, "probability_source": "synthetic_mirror"}], "final_score": 0.5830841121495327, "no_score": 0.42036082474226805, "edge": -0.12191588785046725, "no_edge": 0.125360824742268, "recommendation": "PASS", "confidence_level": "LOW", "signal_type": null, "pass_reason": "YES-side confidence too low: 31% (need \u226567%)", "expected_roi": 0.0, "briefing": {"market": {"id": "1933615", "question": "LoL: EKO Esports vs Aeterna Esports (BO1) - LIT Regular Season", "resolution_criteria": "This market refers to the LoL match between EKO Esports and Aeterna Esports in the LIT Regular Season, initially scheduled for April 10 at 12:00PM ET.\n\nThis market will resolve to \"EKO Esports\" if EKO Esports win the match against Aeterna Esports.\n\nThis market will resolve to \"Aeterna Esports\" if Aeterna Esports win the match against EKO Esports.\n\nIf the match is canceled (not played at all), ends in a tie, or is delayed beyond 7 days from the scheduled date without a winner determined, this market will resolve to 50-50. \n\nIf the match begins but is not completed, and one team wins due to the opponent's forfeiture, disqualification, or walkover, this market will resolve to the team who wins. \n\nIf the match ends in a forfeit, disqualification, or walkover (team withdraws before the start and the other wins automatically), this market will resolve to 50-50. \n\nThe resolution source for this market will be official information from https://gol.gg/esports/home. However, if https://gol.gg/esports/home has not published final results within 2 hours after the event\u2019s conclusion, a consensus of credible reporting may be used instead including video evidence.\n\nIn cases where a team\u2019s listed name includes minor discrepancies from the resolution source, this market will resolve based on the underlying real-world match rather than exact name matching. Recognizable abbreviations, alternate or erroneous spellings, sponsor tags, affiliate or academy designations, regional identifiers, and minor formatting differences will be treated as referring to the same team, provided the intended team can be clearly and uniquely identified within the relevant competition. If a listed team name has no reasonable connection to any participating team, or if it matches or could reasonably refer to another team in the same competition such that the intended team cannot be unambiguously determined, this market will resolve 50-50.", "end_date": "2026-04-10T22:00:00Z", "event_start_time": "2026-04-10T16:00:00Z", "yes_price": 0.705, "volume_usd": 790.5421729999999, "category": "league-of-legends"}, "key_facts": ["The 2026 Esports Nations Cup is an inaugural multi-game, country-based esports tournament held in Riyadh, Saudi Arabia, hosted by the Esports World Cup", "RLCS 2026 - Paris Major: APAC Open 5 is an online Asia-Pacific tournament organized by Epic Games and BLAST.", "This A-Tier tournament takes place from Apr 04", "RLCS 2026 - Paris Major: APAC Open 4 is an online Asia-Pacific tournament organized by Epic Games and BLAST.", "This A-Tier tournament took place from Mar 21", "Find detailed results about the League of Legends match between HMBLE and Aeterna Esports in LIT 2026 Spring that took place on April 3, 2026 - 21:00 CEST."], "news_summary": "Limited information is available about the specific EKO Esports vs Aeterna Esports BO1 match in the LIT Regular Season. Search results confirm that Aeterna Esports is an active team competing in LIT 2026 Spring, as evidenced by their match against HMBLE on April 3, 2026. However, no direct head-to-head data, recent form metrics, or specific performance information about either team in this matchup has been retrieved.", "side_yes_case": ["Aeterna Esports has confirmed participation in LIT 2026 Spring competition", "Insufficient negative evidence about either team's capabilities to discount either opponent"], "side_no_case": ["No performance data retrieved for EKO Esports in current season", "No head-to-head history or matchup analysis available between the two teams"], "key_context": ["Match is part of LIT (Liga Iberoamericana de Ternura/Liga Iberoamericana) Regular Season 2026", "Best-of-1 format reduces sample size for determining stronger team", "Aeterna Esports confirmed active in LIT 2026 Spring as of April 3, 2026", "Insufficient public data on both teams' recent form and roster strength", "No tournament seeding, standings position, or playoff implications data available"], "data_freshness": "2026-04-10T12:09:37.964594Z"}}, "resolution": {"resolved_at": "2026-04-10T22:00:00Z", "winning_side": "YES", "final_yes_price": 1.0, "checked_at": "2026-04-10T20:40:03.579703+00:00", "source": "gamma_api"}, "analysis": null}
{"run_id": "59ca3bfd-9b3b-4f7e-b0e5-943b9728c273", "timestamp": "2026-04-10T12:10:55.126953+00:00", "legacy": false, "result": {"market": {"id": "1911545", "question": "LoL: Skillcamp Esport vs ZYB Esport (BO1) - LFL Regular Season", "resolution_criteria": "This market refers to the LoL match between Skillcamp Esport and ZYB Esport in the LFL Regular Season, initially scheduled for April 10 at 12:00PM ET.\n\nThis market will resolve to \"Skillcamp Esport\" if Skillcamp Esport win the match against ZYB Esport.\n\nThis market will resolve to \"ZYB Esport\" if ZYB Esport win the match against Skillcamp Esport.\n\nIf the match is canceled (not played at all), ends in a tie, or is delayed beyond 7 days from the scheduled date without a winner determined, this market will resolve to 50-50. \n\nIf the match begins but is not completed, and one team wins due to the opponent's forfeiture, disqualification, or walkover, this market will resolve to the team who wins. \n\nIf the match ends in a forfeit, disqualification, or walkover (team withdraws before the start and the other wins automatically), this market will resolve to 50-50. \n\nThe resolution source for this market will be official information from https://gol.gg/esports/home. However, if https://gol.gg/esports/home has not published final results within 2 hours after the event\u2019s conclusion, a consensus of credible reporting may be used instead including video evidence.\n\nIn cases where a team\u2019s listed name includes minor discrepancies from the resolution source, this market will resolve based on the underlying real-world match rather than exact name matching. Recognizable abbreviations, alternate or erroneous spellings, sponsor tags, affiliate or academy designations, regional identifiers, and minor formatting differences will be treated as referring to the same team, provided the intended team can be clearly and uniquely identified within the relevant competition. If a listed team name has no reasonable connection to any participating team, or if it matches or could reasonably refer to another team in the same competition such that the intended team cannot be unambiguously determined, this market will resolve 50-50.", "end_date": "2026-04-10T22:00:00Z", "event_start_time": "2026-04-10T16:00:00Z", "yes_price": 0.55, "volume_usd": 3396.779557999997, "category": "league-of-legends"}, "verdicts": [{"agent_name": "The Rules Lawyer", "probability": 0.68, "confidence_yes": 0.75, "confidence_no": 0.55, "rationale": "Skillcamp dominates on aggregate metrics: 67% win rate (6W-3L) vs ZYB's 30% (3W-7L) is a substantial performance gap. Skillcamp's 66.7% first tower rate indicates superior macro play and objective control, a key predictor of match outcomes. In the head-to-head Game 1 result provided, Skillcamp won decisively, controlling mid-lane damage (39.1% vs 19%) and scaling advantage. ZYB's 80% first blood rate is a misleading stat: it shows early game skirmish capability but has not translated to wins, suggesting poor mid-game execution and inability to close games from advantages. ZYB's jungle (30.1% damage share) did outperform Skillcamp's jungle (7.7%) in the head-to-head example, but this is a single data point and does not overcome Skillcamp's macro dominance. BO1 format reduces Skillcamp's series advantage, but single-game elimination favors teams with stronger overall play and consistency\u2014both metrics favor Skillcamp. Sample sizes are small (9 games for Skillcamp, 10 for ZYB in the provided data), limiting reliability; however, Skillcamp's advantage is consistent across multiple metrics. ZYB's win rate, gold distribution, and damage efficiency are notably weaker across most roles and metrics, suggesting systemic performance gaps rather than one-dimensional weakness. Data references mixed tournament contexts (Invitational Week 3 and Regular Season), introducing slight uncertainty in form assessment, but Skillcamp's dominance is evident across both. No significant injury, roster changes, or external factors noted in briefing; match appears on schedule with no indication of cancellation or forfeit risk. Market price of YES at 55% appears slightly undervalued given Skillcamp's 67% win rate and superior macro metrics; confidence in YES is justified at 68.", "key_factor": "Resolution depends on official gol.gg reporting within 2 hours; no mechanism risk identified given clear win/loss conditions for completed matches.", "probability_yes": 0.68, "probability_no": 0.31999999999999995, "probability_source": "synthetic_mirror"}, {"agent_name": "The Bull", "probability": 0.62, "confidence_yes": 0.75, "confidence_no": 0.5, "rationale": "SKILLCAMP FUNDAMENTALS: 67% win rate (6W-3L) vs ZYB's 30% win rate (3W-7L) represents a 37-percentage-point gap\u2014this is substantial and consistent across recent competition. Win rate is the most reliable predictor of BO1 outcomes. MACRO CONTROL: Skillcamp's 66.7% first tower rate indicates superior objective priority, vision control, and mid-game execution. ZYB's 50% first tower rate (despite 80% first blood) proves ZYB cannot translate skirmish wins into structural advantages. HEAD-TO-HEAD PROOF: The Game 1 matchup shows Skillcamp decisively outscaling ZYB\u2014Skillcamp mid-laner dealt 39.1% team damage vs ZYB mid's 19%. Skillcamp's damage distribution (particularly mid/ADC synergy at 28.8% ADC + 39.1% mid = 67.9%) outpaces ZYB's fragmented output. ZYB'S WEAKNESS: 80% first blood rate is a red flag for inconsistency, not strength. High first blood without corresponding high win rate indicates ZYB wins early fights but loses macro rotations and win conditions. This is precisely what BO1 punishes\u2014one mistake in scaling phase ends the game. GOLD EFFICIENCY: Skillcamp maintains better gold distribution ratios across roles (mid 24.6%, ADC 27.2%) vs ZYB (mid 19%, ADC 26.9%), showing better scaling setup. ZYB's jungle takes 22.3% gold but only contributes 30.1% damage\u2014inefficient resource allocation. UNDERDOG'S LIMITATION: BO1 format removes series comeback potential but does NOT favor upsets when one team is structurally superior. ZYB would need to replicate their 80% first blood rate AND execute a win condition better than they have in 7 losses\u2014sample size suggests this is unreliable. UNCERTAINTY ON CONFIDENCE_NO: ZYB's high first blood rate is legitimate and single-game variance in BO1 exists, but briefing lacks sufficient ZYB roster strength data, recent form trends, or coaching/draft advantages to build a credible NO case. Confidence_no is held at 50 due to BO1 inherent variance and missing team composition intel. SAMPLE SIZE CAVEAT: Both teams' records span LFL 2026 Spring + Invitational weeks; small sample means win rates can shift. However, Skillcamp's 6W-3L is derived from visible matches and ZYB's 3W-7L is consistent\u2014this 2:1 ratio is defensible for a BO1 favorite. MARKET PRICING VALIDATION: YES at 55% is actually underpriced given Skillcamp's 67% win rate and 37-point gap over ZYB. The market may be overweighting BO1 variance or ZYB's first blood metric. Fair odds suggest 60-65% is more appropriate.", "key_factor": "Skillcamp's dominant 67% win rate, superior macro execution (66.7% first tower), and proven head-to-head victory demonstrate clear structural superiority over a ZYB team that cannot convert early advantages despite high first blood rate.", "probability_yes": 0.62, "probability_no": 0.38, "probability_source": "synthetic_mirror"}, {"agent_name": "The Bear", "probability": 0.38, "confidence_yes": 0.72, "confidence_no": 0.55, "rationale": "SKILLCAMP FAVORED CASE: 67% win rate (6W-3L) is substantially higher than ZYB's 30% (3W-7L); 66.7% first tower rate shows macro dominance; mid-lane damage output (39.1% vs 19%) in head-to-head suggests superior scaling and teamfight positioning. ZYB UPSET POTENTIAL - EARLY GAME AGGRESSION: 80% first blood rate is elite and indicates consistent early skirmish dominance; this was demonstrated in head-to-head where ZYB's jungle (30.1% damage) vastly outperformed Skillcamp's jungle (7.7% damage), suggesting ZYB found a playable draft/execution route. BO1 FORMAT AMPLIFIES VOLATILITY: Single elimination removes best-of-series safety nets; one early teamfight loss, one jungle gank sequence, or one draft counter can snowball irreversibly; Skillcamp's macro-heavy, scaling-focused playstyle is vulnerable to early-game collapse in BO1 where recovery windows are narrow. ZYB'S OBJECTIVE CONVERSION GAP: Despite 80% first blood, ZYB only secures 50% first tower\u2014gap indicates poor mid-game pivot and teamfight follow-up after early kills; however, in a BO1 where Skillcamp must respect the first blood threat, ZYB's high-pressure early game may create permanent map control advantage. SAMPLE SIZE AND TOURNAMENT CONTEXT CONCERNS: Both teams show mixed results across LFL Invitational Week 3 and Spring Regular Season; small sample sizes (9 games for Skillcamp, 10 for ZYB in visible data) reduce reliability of win rate metrics; recent form is unclear from briefing due to data fragmentation across multiple tournaments. SKILLCAMP VULNERABILITY IN JUNGLE: 7.7% jungle damage share is dangerously low and suggests weak jungle synergy or prioritization; ZYB's 30.1% jungle damage in head-to-head indicates their jungler can exert map pressure Skillcamp struggles to match or counter; this is exploitable in BO1 where early jungle lead cascades. HEAD-TO-HEAD RESULT INTERPRETATION: Single Game 1 data shows Skillcamp won but does NOT show the conditions under which ZYB won the games ZYB took (if any); incomplete head-to-head dataset limits confidence in trend identification. GOLD DISTRIBUTION AND WIN CLOSURE: Skillcamp's superior gold distribution (27.2% ADC vs ZYB's 26.9%) is marginal; gold doesn't guarantee execution; ZYB's 50% first tower rate despite low win rate suggests ZYB trades resources but fails at close-out\u2014however, in BO1, a single successful close with early advantage ends the game. UNDERDOG'S EDGE IN BO1: Skillcamp as favorite is optimized for 5-game series where consistency and macro control accumulate; ZYB as underdog benefits from BO1 variance, upside surprise picks, and the fact that one early game win condition (which their first blood rate suggests they can create) is sufficient to claim the match. CONFIDENCE NOTES: Confidence in YES is moderate-high (72) because Skillcamp's win rate gap and macro metrics are clear; however, confidence in NO is lower (55) because briefing provides limited visibility into ZYB's recent regular season form, underlying team mechanics, and whether their first blood rate is sustainable or context-dependent; data gaps prevent definitive NO evaluation.", "key_factor": "ZYB's elite 80% first blood rate and jungle dominance (30.1% damage vs Skillcamp's 7.7%) in head-to-head play demonstrates a blueprint for early aggression that can exploit Skillcamp's macro-focused, scaling-dependent playstyle in a single BO1 where early mistakes are irreversible.", "probability_yes": 0.38, "probability_no": 0.62, "probability_source": "synthetic_mirror"}, {"agent_name": "The Base-Rate Statistician", "probability": 0.68, "confidence_yes": 0.72, "confidence_no": 0.45, "rationale": "SKILLCAMP STRENGTHS: 67% win rate (6W-3L) vs ZYB's 30% (3W-7L) represents a 37-percentage-point gap, substantially above historical noise. First tower rate of 66.7% demonstrates macro control and objective priority execution. SKILLCAMP MACRO EFFICIENCY: Mid-lane damage share (39.1% vs ZYB 19%) and superior gold distribution efficiency (27.2% ADC vs ZYB 26.9%, balanced across roles) indicate better scaling and win-condition closure. HEAD-TO-HEAD PRECEDENT: Game 1 result shows Skillcamp victory with dominant mid-lane scaling advantage. BO1 format does not erase demonstrated skill differential\u2014it merely removes series reset opportunities. ZYB EARLY AGGRESSION: 80% first blood rate is notable but CONTRADICTED by 30% overall win rate, indicating systematic failure to convert early advantages into wins. This is a red flag for macro execution, not a pathway to victory. ZYB JUNGLE COUNTER-ARGUMENT: Jungle 30.1% damage share exceeded Skillcamp's 7.7% in Game 1, but this occurred within Skillcamp's overall victory. Single-role outperformance does not predict team-level outcome. BO1 UPSET RISK: BO1 format does increase variance and upset probability vs best-of-series. However, a 37-point win-rate gap is large enough that even with BO1 variance, the favorite remains heavily favored (~68-72% range). SAMPLE SIZE LIMITATION: Both teams' records are small (9-10 matches visible). This increases confidence intervals around true strength estimates but does not eliminate the directional signal (Skillcamp >> ZYB). TOURNAMENT CONTEXT: Data mixes LFL 2026 Spring Regular Season and Invitational Week 3 results. Invitational context (recent BO3 elimination for some teams) may introduce form variance, but Skillcamp's Regular Season win rate (67%) aligns with overall standing. MARKET PRICING (55% YES): Current market price of 55% on YES is BELOW the historical base rate for a team with a 37-point win-rate advantage in BO1 format. This suggests modest undervaluation of Skillcamp or market uncertainty (low volume: $3,397). CONFIDENCE ASYMMETRY: High confidence in YES is justified by clear win-rate gap and objective metrics. Lower confidence in NO reflects inability to identify concrete ZYB path to victory beyond generic BO1 upset variance.", "key_factor": "In BO1 esports matches, the higher-seeded/favored team (67% win rate vs 30%) wins approximately 70-75% of the time; upsets in single-game formats occur ~25-30% of the time.", "probability_yes": 0.68, "probability_no": 0.31999999999999995, "probability_source": "synthetic_mirror"}, {"agent_name": "The Sentiment Analyst", "probability": 0.68, "confidence_yes": 0.72, "confidence_no": 0.55, "rationale": "\u2022 Skillcamp's 67% win rate vs ZYB's 30% is stark and reliable across sample. Market price of 55% YES appears to be UNDERWEIGHTING this disparity. \u2022 Skillcamp's 66.7% first tower rate vs ZYB's 50% indicates superior macro control and objective execution\u2014a reliable predictor in LoL. Market seems to discount this edge. \u2022 Head-to-head Game 1 showed Skillcamp dominance: mid-lane damage 39.1% vs 19%, superior gold distribution, and clean scaling. Single BO1 has noise, but this is directional evidence. \u2022 ZYB's 80% first blood rate is a RED FLAG, not strength: it shows aggression without conversion to wins. This is classic 'getting ahead then losing'\u2014indicates systemic mid-game weakness, not clutch potential. \u2022 BO1 format does favor upsets, but ZYB would need to overcome a ~37 percentage-point win-rate gap. This is not a typical underdog scenario; it's a low-tier team vs dominant team. \u2022 Jungle performance in Game 1: ZYB jungle 30.1% damage vs Skillcamp jungle 7.7% is misleading\u2014Skillcamp's mid-laner (39.1% DMG) shows resource allocation strategy, not weakness. ZYB's jungle had to carry because lanes lost. \u2022 Market at 55% YES is pricing in ~45% for ZYB. For a 30% win-rate team to have 45% equity in a BO1, market is implicitly saying 'BO1 variance negates 37 WR points.' This is overcorrecting. \u2022 No major injuries, roster changes, or meta shifts mentioned in briefing. Skillcamp form is stable; ZYB form is consistently poor. \u2022 Crowd sentiment: Market appears RATIONALLY calibrated but slightly UNDERVALUING Skillcamp's dominance. Price reflects 'BO1 can surprise' narrative without weighting recent win-rate gap heavily enough. \u2022 Confidence in YES is higher (72) than confidence in NO (55) because Skillcamp's metrics are clear and multi-dimensional (WR, first tower, head-to-head). ZYB's case rests on BO1 variance and a single jungle stat\u2014weaker foundation.", "key_factor": "Rational", "probability_yes": 0.68, "probability_no": 0.31999999999999995, "probability_source": "synthetic_mirror"}, {"agent_name": "The Domain Specialist", "probability": 0.68, "confidence_yes": 0.72, "confidence_no": 0.55, "rationale": "Win rate differential is stark: Skillcamp 67% (6W-3L) vs ZYB 30% (3W-7L) represents ~2.2x performance gap, substantive in esports where consistency compounds. Skillcamp's 66.7% first tower rate directly correlates to macro dominance and resource conversion\u2014this is a leading indicator of win probability in LoL, not merely coincidental. ZYB's 80% first blood rate without corresponding win rate indicates a strategic or execution problem post-15min: likely poor mid-game teamfighting, objective pivoting failure, or win condition identification. Head-to-head Game 1 data shows Skillcamp mid-laner (39.1% DMG share vs ZYB's 19%) controlling damage narrative\u2014LoL scaling is non-linear; mid advantage compounds exponentially. BO1 format does reduce margin for error, but does not eliminate structural team weaknesses; ZYB would need to execute perfectly to overcome the macro deficit. ZYB's jungle (30.1% DMG) outperforming in H2H is notable but represents one game; Skillcamp's 7.7% jungle DMG in that context suggests Skillcamp played a controlled macro game, not a jungler deficit. Skillcamp's gold distribution (more balanced across ADC 27.2%, Mid 24.6%) vs ZYB's imbalance (ADC 26.9%, Jungle 22.3%) suggests better team cohesion and scaling. LFL 2026 Spring tournament context: Small sample sizes (9-10 games per team) limit statistical confidence; however, consistent 67% vs 30% gap across multiple opponents is non-random. No recent roster changes, patches, or red-flag forfeit/disqualification risks noted in briefing; match resolution criteria straightforward. Confidence in NO side reduced due to insufficient counter-evidence: ZYB's only demonstrable strength (first blood) is not predictive of match victory at ~30% win rate; no credible path to exploit identified beyond hoping for variance.", "key_factor": "ZYB's 80% first blood rate with only 30% win rate indicates severe mid-game execution collapse\u2014a structural weakness that BO1 format cannot fully mitigate, despite early aggression being valuable in single-game scenarios.", "probability_yes": 0.68, "probability_no": 0.31999999999999995, "probability_source": "synthetic_mirror"}, {"agent_name": "The Contrarian", "probability": 0.62, "confidence_yes": 0.72, "confidence_no": 0.45, "rationale": "Skillcamp's dominant 67% win rate and 66.7% first-tower control strongly favor a YES outcome, but BO1 format inherently amplifies upset risk \u2014 small execution windows matter disproportionately. ZYB's 80% first-blood rate is conspicuously high and inconsistent with their 30% win rate, suggesting a team designed for early aggression that routinely fails to convert leads \u2014 this creates either a 'style-matchup' variable or a real execution gap that could flip on a specific draft. Head-to-head data shows Skillcamp won Game 1 decisively with mid-lane dominance (39.1% damage vs 19%), but ZYB's jungle (30.1% damage) outpaced Skillcamp's jungle (7.7%), indicating a potential weak point in Skillcamp's playstyle if exploited. Briefing is silent on recent roster changes, player substitutions, health/availability updates, or meta-patch shifts since the last match (April 9) \u2014 any unmentioned last-minute lineup change (e.g., backup jungler, mid-laner benching) could catastrophically alter predictions. ZYB's high first-blood rate + poor conversion suggests drafting or champion-pool flexibility around early skirmish champions; if LFL meta has shifted toward early-game centric comps post-patch 16.3, ZYB's playstyle becomes less exploitable. Sample sizes are small; Skillcamp's 6W-3L and ZYB's 3W-7L come from ~9 matches each, insufficient to rule out variance. A single upset in this small sample does not refute skill difference but does indicate predictability risk. Skillcamp's mid-laner (39.1% damage in Game 1) appears to be a primary win condition; if ZYB has scouted a targeted counter-pick or if Skillcamp's mid-laner is fatigued/distracted by back-to-back matches (tournament context unclear), this scaling edge evaporates. Invitational context in briefing suggests recent high-stakes play; morale and motivation asymmetry could favor ZYB if they are playing loose/fearless as underdogs while Skillcamp bears 'favorite's curse' pressure in a single-elimination format. Market price (55% YES / 45% NO) implies ~10 percentage-point gap; given Skillcamp's metrics, probability should lean toward YES, but BO1 variance and ZYB's unresolved first-blood-to-win conversion problem warrant moderate confidence reduction on the YES side.", "key_factor": "ZYB's jungle (Sleyer) exploits a surprise early-game meta shift or pocket pick that neutalizes Skillcamp's mid-laner scaling advantage, converting ZYB's 80% first-blood rate into a suffocating early-mid game tempo that Skillcamp cannot match despite superior late-game win conditions.", "probability_yes": 0.62, "probability_no": 0.38, "probability_source": "synthetic_mirror"}], "final_score": 0.6203529411764707, "no_score": 0.3816666666666667, "edge": 0.07035294117647062, "no_edge": -0.06833333333333325, "recommendation": "BUY YES", "confidence_level": "HIGH", "signal_type": "HIGH VALUE", "pass_reason": "", "expected_roi": 0.818181818181818, "briefing": {"market": {"id": "1911545", "question": "LoL: Skillcamp Esport vs ZYB Esport (BO1) - LFL Regular Season", "resolution_criteria": "This market refers to the LoL match between Skillcamp Esport and ZYB Esport in the LFL Regular Season, initially scheduled for April 10 at 12:00PM ET.\n\nThis market will resolve to \"Skillcamp Esport\" if Skillcamp Esport win the match against ZYB Esport.\n\nThis market will resolve to \"ZYB Esport\" if ZYB Esport win the match against Skillcamp Esport.\n\nIf the match is canceled (not played at all), ends in a tie, or is delayed beyond 7 days from the scheduled date without a winner determined, this market will resolve to 50-50. \n\nIf the match begins but is not completed, and one team wins due to the opponent's forfeiture, disqualification, or walkover, this market will resolve to the team who wins. \n\nIf the match ends in a forfeit, disqualification, or walkover (team withdraws before the start and the other wins automatically), this market will resolve to 50-50. \n\nThe resolution source for this market will be official information from https://gol.gg/esports/home. However, if https://gol.gg/esports/home has not published final results within 2 hours after the event\u2019s conclusion, a consensus of credible reporting may be used instead including video evidence.\n\nIn cases where a team\u2019s listed name includes minor discrepancies from the resolution source, this market will resolve based on the underlying real-world match rather than exact name matching. Recognizable abbreviations, alternate or erroneous spellings, sponsor tags, affiliate or academy designations, regional identifiers, and minor formatting differences will be treated as referring to the same team, provided the intended team can be clearly and uniquely identified within the relevant competition. If a listed team name has no reasonable connection to any participating team, or if it matches or could reasonably refer to another team in the same competition such that the intended team cannot be unambiguously determined, this market will resolve 50-50.", "end_date": "2026-04-10T22:00:00Z", "event_start_time": "2026-04-10T16:00:00Z", "yes_price": 0.55, "volume_usd": 3396.779557999997, "category": "league-of-legends"}, "key_facts": ["Find detailed results about the League of Legends match between Skillcamp and Esprit Sh\u014dnen in LFL 2026 Spring that took place on April 9, 2026 - 18:00", "Picks, Blue Side, Red Side, Bans, Picks & Bans.", "Champion, \u2211, W, L, WR, %T, \u2211, W, L, WR, \u2211, W, L, WR, \u2211, %T, \u2211, %T, Details.", "| Skillcamp | |\n --- |\n| Skillcamp | |\n| Win Rate | |  |  |  