ists. VP has not shown such a shutout loss. If VP studied or prepared for that comp weakness, Geekay is susceptible. NARROW MARGIN PARADOX: Geekay's Apr 10 playoff win was 3-2 (one map from loss). VP's Apr 4 regular-season win was 3-1 (more dominant). In BO3 format, a team that won 3-1 in round-robin often carries better map-read discipline into knockout than a 3-2 playoff scraper. PLAYOFFS VS REGULAR SEASON FORM: Both teams qualified (top 4). Lower bracket positioning suggests neither is favored; competitive parity is genuine. Market is pricing 54/46 as near coin-flip, which is reasonable, but the +18pp swing on a single 3-2 match is reactionary. BO3 FORMAT RISK: Lower bracket finals BO3 (not BO5) reduce outlier variance. Teams that excel in BO3 usually have strong map-pool consistency. Geekay's scattered recent results (0-3 loss, then 3-0, then 3-2, then 0-3 to TM in semis Apr 11) suggest inconsistent map selection or meta adaptation. MOMENTUM ILLUSION: Market sees 'Geekay won last match' and extrapolates. But Geekay then lost 0-3 to Twisted Minds on Apr 11 in the upper-bracket semis (per briefing, April 11 match vs TM 0:3). This is a MASSIVE red flag: a team that beat VP 3-2, then lost to TM 0-3 two days later, has serious form volatility. ACTUAL MARKET CONTEXT: This is a LOWER bracket final (Geekay and VP both lost their upper-bracket matches). Geekay's Apr 10 victory sent them to lower final; they should have been confident momentum. But the Apr 11 0-3 loss to TM means Geekay has now lost TWO matches in 36 hours. Losing streaks in esports correlate with mental tilt. CONFIDENCE ASYMMETRY: Strong case for NO (VP's consistency, Geekay's volatility, mental fatigue post-shutout) is well-supported by match records. Case for YES (recent head-to-head win, qualifier depth) relies on single recency data point (+18pp on Apr 10 match). This is a classic misprice favoring recency. MISSING INTEL: Briefing does not report injury, illness, or roster changes in the 48 hours bef