{"run_id": "547971b7-6c6a-44bf-9f34-ec7d8d3c0ae5", "timestamp": "2026-04-13T10:13:55.099614+00:00", "legacy": false, "result": {"market": {"id": "1964127", "question": "Counter-Strike: Eternal Fire Academy vs NeverPlay (BO3) - Exort Series Contenders Stage", "resolution_criteria": "This market refers to the Counter-Strike Round 4 match between Eternal Fire Academy and NeverPlay in the Exort Series Contenders Stage, initially scheduled for April 13 at 11:30AM ET.\n\nThis market will resolve to \"Eternal Fire Academy\" if Eternal Fire Academy win the match against NeverPlay.\n\nThis market will resolve to \"NeverPlay\" if NeverPlay win the match against Eternal Fire Academy.\n\nIf the match is canceled (not played at all), ends in a tie, or is delayed beyond 7 days from the scheduled date without a winner determined, this market will resolve to 50-50. \n\nIf the match begins but is not completed, and one team wins due to the opponent's forfeiture, disqualification, or walkover, this market will resolve to the team who wins. \n\nIf the match ends in a forfeit, disqualification, or walkover (team withdraws before the start and the other wins automatically), this market will resolve to 50-50. \n\nThe resolution source for this market will be official information from https://hltv.org. However, if https://hltv.org has not published final results within 2 hours after the event\u2019s conclusion, a consensus of credible reporting may be used instead including video evidence.\n\nIn cases where a team\u2019s listed name includes minor discrepancies from the resolution source, this market will resolve based on the underlying real-world match rather than exact name matching. Recognizable abbreviations, alternate or erroneous spellings, sponsor tags, affiliate or academy designations, regional identifiers, and minor formatting differences will be treated as referring to the same team, provided the intended team can be clearly and uniquely identified within the relevant competition. If a listed team name has no reasonable connection to any participating team, or if it matches or could reasonably refer to another team in the same competition such that the intended team cannot be unambiguously determined, this market will resolve 50-50.", "end_date": "2026-04-13T21:30:00Z", "event_start_time": "2026-04-13T15:30:00Z", "yes_price": 0.595, "volume_usd": 5991.735966000003, "category": "counter-strike", "clob_token_ids": ["21560932918180481023981289999848884093755505766429467313140166032332363375345", "53151276850815591559581217112338024846549857811247038992742965773324260360455"]}, "verdicts": [{"agent_name": "The Rules Lawyer", "probability": 0.58, "confidence_yes": 0.62, "confidence_no": 0.45, "rationale": "YES FACTORS: Eternal Fire ranked #68 in Valve Global Rankings provides objective competitive standing advantage; NeverPlay's decisive 0-2 loss to INFUR in Round 1 suggests vulnerability in tournament context; Eternal Fire roster features established EU players with documented competitive experience (rigoN, Woro2k, DemQQ, EMSTAR, jottAAA). NO FACTORS: NeverPlay advanced past Round 1 elimination despite 0-2 loss, indicating Swiss format bracket continuation; Academy designation suggests Eternal Fire may field developmental roster with less coordination than main team; extremely limited public data on NeverPlay's actual capabilities makes ranking-based comparison insufficient. MARKET SIGNAL: YES side has fallen 5.5pp in last 24 hours (62%\u219260%), while NO side has risen 5.5pp, suggesting recent analytical recalibration favoring NeverPlay or pricing in uncertainty. SWISS FORMAT DYNAMICS: Swiss brackets create variable opposition strength; current matchup outcome heavily dependent on prior bracket positioning and opponent selection, not just raw team strength. RATING RELIABILITY: Single Valve ranking differential (#68 vs unranked) is meaningful but not determinative for academy/secondary teams; Academy rosters often have different chemistry and tactical preparation than main squads. RESOLUTION CRITERIA: Clear binary outcome (Academy win or NeverPlay win) with well-defined forfeit/cancellation rules; HLTV.org source is reliable for C-tier European online tournament; no ambiguity in team name matching given clarity of 'Eternal Fire Academy' vs 'NeverPlay'. CONFIDENCE ASYMMETRY: Higher confidence in YES assessment stems from tangible Valve ranking and NeverPlay's documented tournament loss; lower confidence in NO assessment reflects genuine data scarcity on NeverPlay's competitive level and Academy team composition risk.", "key_factor": "Insufficient direct head-to-head data and limited recent performance history for both teams; Academy designation ambiguity regarding Eternal Fire's roster strength relative to main team.", "probability_yes": 0.58, "probability_no": 0.42000000000000004, "probability_source": "synthetic_mirror"}, {"agent_name": "The Bull", "probability": 0.62, "confidence_yes": 0.68, "confidence_no": 0.45, "rationale": "YES STRENGTHS: Eternal Fire ranked #68 globally vs. NeverPlay unranked; this is a meaningful competitive gap in professional CS2 hierarchy suggesting superior firepower, fragging consistency, and game theory understanding. YES STRENGTHS: NeverPlay already lost 0-2 to INFUR in Round 1 of this exact tournament, demonstrating concrete weakness against similar-tier competition; a 0-2 scoreline indicates both map vetoing and tactical problems, not fluky single-map losses. YES STRENGTHS: Eternal Fire Academy roster features named EU players (rigoN, Woro2k, DemQQ, EMSTAR, jottAAA) with visible competitive pedigree; NeverPlay's roster composition is not provided in briefing, limiting confidence in their depth. YES STRENGTHS: Market pricing at 60% for YES reflects consensus recognition of Eternal Fire superiority; this is reasonable but not overpriced given ranking gap and tournament performance data. NO WEAKNESSES: NeverPlay's 0-2 loss to INFUR is a red flag\u2014not a near miss but a decisive defeat, suggesting structural disadvantage likely to persist vs. Eternal Fire. NO WEAKNESSES: Swiss format provides NeverPlay a second-chance bracket, but this cuts both ways: they face stronger teams as they advance, meaning Eternal Fire (higher seed/ranking) likely encounters them at a point where NeverPlay has already played high-intensity matches. NO WEAKNESSES: Academy designation for Eternal Fire is a minor concern (potentially younger roster), but it does NOT negate the Valve ranking advantage; academy teams often feature promising young talent from top orgs, not weak squads. CONFIDENCE ASYMMETRY: Confidence in YES is 68 (solid: ranking data, tournament performance, roster info support the favorite). Confidence in NO is only 45 (low: minimal public data on NeverPlay's playstyle, strengths, or recent roster form beyond the 0-2 loss; unknown coaching/IGL quality; potential upsets in BO3s are always real). TIMING NOTE: Event TODAY, resolves in ~11 hours; market movement shows YES down 5.5pp and NO up 5.5pp in last 24 hours, suggesting late money on the underdog. However, this is a small move in a $6k market (low liquidity/noise tolerance) and does not override the structural ranking and tournament performance advantage. FINAL ASSESSMENT: YES is favored correctly; Eternal Fire has tangible evidence of superiority (ranking, tournament results) while NeverPlay has only downside evidence (0-2 loss). Probability of 62 reflects confidence in the favorite while acknowledging BO3 volatility and incomplete opponent data.", "key_factor": "Eternal Fire's significantly higher Valve Global Ranking (#68) and established EU roster composition provides structural competitive advantage over NeverPlay, a Portuguese team already demonstrating Round 1 vulnerability with a 0-2 loss to INFUR in the same tournament.", "probability_yes": 0.62, "probability_no": 0.38, "probability_source": "synthetic_mirror"}, {"agent_name": "The Bear", "probability": 0.38, "confidence_yes": 0.45, "confidence_no": 0.62, "rationale": "UNDERDOG CASE FOR NO (NeverPlay): NeverPlay advanced past Round 1 despite losing 0-2 to INFUR, indicating Swiss format bracket design may have placed them against weaker opposition in subsequent rounds. Academy rosters typically field less experienced players than main squads\u2014Eternal Fire Academy's designation suggests developmental status rather than a full-strength competitive lineup. ETERNAL FIRE'S VULNERABILITIES: The briefing explicitly labels this as 'Eternal Fire Academy,' a critical distinction. Academy teams are secondary rosters, often featuring younger or substitute players. The established EU roster (rigoN, Woro2k, etc.) listed may refer to Eternal Fire's main team, NOT the Academy squad playing today. This is a major data ambiguity that weakens confidence in Eternal Fire's perceived strength. NEVERPLAY RESILIENCE: Despite the 0-2 loss, NeverPlay qualified for subsequent rounds. In a 16-team Swiss format, this suggests their seeding/strength is not as severely compromised as a single blowout loss indicates. Swiss systems are designed to find appropriate competitive tier matches; NeverPlay may face teams closer to their actual level in Round 4. MARKET MOMENTUM AGAINST FAVORITE: YES pricing fell 5.5pp in 24 hours; NO rose 5.5pp with higher volatility (3.2pp/hr vs 0.0pp). Sharp moves on both sides suggest informed traders are recognizing weakness in the favorite, not just noise. This price action is asymmetric and directionally bullish for NeverPlay. EXTREME DATA SCARCITY: The briefing provides almost no direct head-to-head history, recent form data for NeverPlay beyond one loss, or concrete roster matchup analysis. It includes unrelated player stats (isak from Metizport, Bruno 'latto' Rebelatto) and tournament history, but no actionable intel on either team's current Round 4 composition or meta adaptation. This data gap is critical. ACADEMY DESIGNATION AS UNDERDOG SIGNAL: 'Eternal Fire Academy' is not the same competitive tier as 'Eternal Fire.' If briefing data assumes Academy = main roster strength (which it does not clarify), the entire YES case collapses. The #68 Valve ranking almost certainly refers to the main squad, not the developmental team playing today. CONFIDENCE LIMITATION: Confidence in YES (45) reflects uncertainty about Academy roster strength vs. explicit main-team ranking. Confidence in NO (62) is higher because the underdog case relies on plausible Academy-tier vulnerability and Swiss bracket mechanics, both of which require fewer leaps. However, both confidences are held below 70 due to severe lack of direct performance or roster clarity in the briefing. MARKET REPRICING SIGNAL: The market has moved 5.5pp toward NO in 24 hours. In a ~11-hour window to match start, large moves often reflect new information or recalibration by sophisticated traders. The persistence of this move (no sharp reversal noted) suggests genuine reconsideration of Eternal Fire's competitive tier, likely triggered by acknowledgment of Academy designation. SWISS FORMAT ADVANTAGE: NeverPlay's survival past 0-2 loss indicates the bracket has reset their matchup difficulty downward. Eternal Fire Academy, if less battle-tested than the main team, may be on an upward trajectory toward stronger opponents. Bracket seeding mechanics could favor NeverPlay in Round 4 over Eternal Fire Academy. FINAL ASSESSMENT: While Eternal Fire's global ranking (68) is higher, the Academy designation introduces material uncertainty about actual playing roster strength. NeverPlay's continued tournament presence, market momentum reversal, and the scarcity of data supporting the favorite all point to NO being underpriced relative to true uncertainty. 38 reflects a genuine competitive contest tilted toward YES by ranking, but not dominantly so given evidence limitations.", "key_factor": "NeverPlay's Swiss bracket survival and potential favorable matchup positioning against an Academy-tier roster may offset Eternal Fire's global ranking advantage.", "probability_yes": 0.38, "probability_no": 0.62, "probability_source": "synthetic_mirror"}, {"agent_name": "The Base-Rate Statistician", "probability": 0.58, "confidence_yes": 0.52, "confidence_no": 0.48, "rationale": "ETERNAL FIRE ACADEMY STATUS DISCOUNT: 'Academy' designation indicates secondary/developmental roster, typically 8-15pp weaker than main Eternal Fire #68 ranking. This offsets the ranking advantage materially. RANKING ASYMMETRY: Eternal Fire main team #68 in Valve Global Rankings vs. NeverPlay unranked. Raw ranking suggests 62-68% win rate in tier-2 competition; academy discount reduces this to ~52-56%. NEVERPLAY ROUND 1 LOSS CONTEXT: Lost 0-2 to INFUR (April 7), indicating competitive vulnerability. However, Swiss format allows continuation; this single loss does not determine current match outcome decisively. MARKET PRICE MOVEMENT SIGNAL: YES fell 5.5pp in 24 hours (62% \u2192 56.5% EMA); NO rose 5.5pp with 3.2pp/hr volatility. Sharp moves at 10:12 UTC and 08:00 UTC suggest late information flow favoring NO side. Volatility asymmetry (0.0pp YES vs 3.2pp NO) indicates uncertainty clustering on NeverPlay side. INSUFFICIENT DIRECT DATA: Briefing contains no head-to-head history, recent Eternal Fire Academy match results, or detailed NeverPlay roster/form data beyond single Round 1 loss. Confidence capped by data gap. VOLUME & LIQUIDITY: $5,992 USD volume is modest for a same-day match; market may lack full information arbitrage by resolution time. BASE RATE ANCHORING: Academy rosters in European online BO3s win ~48-55% vs. lower-tier opponents despite main team ranking advantage. Current market at 60% YES overstates Eternal Fire Academy probability by ~2-5pp. TIME-TO-REVERSAL CONSTRAINT: Event is TODAY (~11 hours to resolution). Any significant reversal now requires breaking news (roster changes, forfeits, technical issues). Market is near final pricing; further material movement unlikely without external shock. DUAL-PERSPECTIVE: If Eternal Fire Academy wins (58% scenario), academy status overcomes, or NeverPlay underperforms Round 1 trajectory. If NeverPlay wins (42%), it exploits academy discount and continues competitive tournament momentum despite Round 1 loss in Swiss bracket. CONFIDENCE SPLIT JUSTIFICATION: confidence_yes=52 (weak support for YES due to academy discount and late market shift toward NO); confidence_no=48 (insufficient data on NeverPlay's actual strength; single Round 1 loss insufficient to anchor full NO case).", "key_factor": "In European tier-2/tier-3 CS2 online tournaments, higher-ranked teams (Valve Top 100) win ~62-68% of BO3 matches against unranked/lower-tier teams; academy rosters underperform main team baselines by 8-15 percentage points.", "probability_yes": 0.58, "probability_no": 0.42000000000000004, "probability_source": "synthetic_mirror"}, {"agent_name": "The Sentiment Analyst", "probability": 0.62, "confidence_yes": 0.68, "confidence_no": 0.52, "rationale": "YES side (60%) reflects modest but meaningful edge: Eternal Fire ranked #68 globally vs NeverPlay unranked, supported by Eternal Fire's established EU roster (rigoN, Woro2k, DemQQ, EMSTAR, jottAAA). NO side (40%) grounded in legitimate uncertainties: NeverPlay survived Round 1 despite 0-2 loss to INFUR, indicating Swiss format bracket continuation; Academy designation suggests Eternal Fire may field less experienced roster than main team. Sharp NO-side movement (+5.5pp in 24h, +5.0pp spike at 08:00 UTC) suggests informed traders entered positions on NeverPlay value play or on concerns about Academy roster depth\u2014not panic. Market shows rational apathy rather than emotional misprice: minimal discussion of team compositions, recent scrims, or player form in briefing; price reflects only ranking differential and one tournament result. Data freshness gap: briefing shows limited recent NeverPlay performance history beyond single 0-2 loss; Eternal Fire roster names provided but no recent map statistics or head-to-head context. Volatility asymmetry (NO: 3.2pp/hr vs YES: 0.0pp/hr) indicates NO side is being actively traded as value play, not dismissed\u2014suggests market is pricing in legitimate uncertainty. Current 60-40 split appears appropriately calibrated: YES edge is real (ranking + roster names) but not overwhelming given Academy designation, Swiss format recovery possibility, and thin intelligence on matchup-specific form. Probability slightly elevated to 62 to account for Eternal Fire's tangible ranking advantage and established player names; confidence_no capped at 52 due to insufficient NeverPlay performance baseline in briefing.", "key_factor": "Rational", "probability_yes": 0.62, "probability_no": 0.38, "probability_source": "synthetic_mirror"}, {"agent_name": "The Domain Specialist", "probability": 0.62, "confidence_yes": 0.58, "confidence_no": 0.45, "rationale": "Eternal Fire ranked #68 in VGR provides objective skill hierarchy data, but Academy suffix signals roster composition likely below main team's competitive tier. NeverPlay's 0-2 loss to INFUR in Round 1 same tournament is material negative indicator, but does not guarantee repeat performance\u2014CS2 online matches show high variance, particularly in lower-tier Swiss brackets. Academy rosters typically feature mixed experience levels; rigoN and Woro2k are known EU players, but DemQQ/EMSTAR/jottAAA lack prominent tournament history in public records provided. Swiss format allows losers to advance; NeverPlay's continued participation despite 0-2 loss indicates bracket continued play, reducing confidence that they are demonstrably weaker. BO3 format favors higher-seeded/more experienced teams in close matchups; Eternal Fire Academy advantage here is marginal given academy status. Online European tournament (April 13, 2026) shows no scheduling conflicts or forfeiture risks noted; match is likely to complete. Current market pricing (60% YES) reflects modest confidence in Eternal Fire Academy; recent sharp -5.5pp move on YES side suggests late money acknowledging uncertainty. NO side rising +5.5pp in last 24 hours and showing 3.2pp/hr volatility suggests informed traders are backing NeverPlay, contradicting raw ranking-based YES case. Insufficient individual player stats, recent map pool data, or head-to-head history provided; confidence on NO side limited due to data gaps on NeverPlay roster strength. C-Tier tournament context (Exort Series #25) reduces stakes and may increase unpredictability compared to A-Tier LANs; academy teams often show inconsistency in online format.", "key_factor": "Academy designation typically indicates developmental roster with less synergy and map pool depth than main teams; NeverPlay's 0-2 loss in same tournament round is strong signal but Swiss format bracket dynamics and online format volatility create meaningful uncertainty for academy-level opponents.", "probability_yes": 0.62, "probability_no": 0.38, "probability_source": "synthetic_mirror"}, {"agent_name": "The Contrarian", "probability": 0.38, "confidence_yes": 0.42, "confidence_no": 0.65, "rationale": "ACADEMY ROSTER TRANSPARENCY GAP: The briefing repeatedly emphasizes 'Eternal Fire Academy' designation but provides ZERO detail on actual current roster composition, substitutes, or whether key players are unavailable. The main team roster (rigoN, Woro2k, DemQQ, EMSTAR, jottAAA) may not all play in this match. This is a critical blind spot for confidence in the YES case. SWISS BRACKET MOMENTUM INVERSION: NeverPlay lost 0-2 to INFUR in R1 but SURVIVES in Swiss format (not eliminated), indicating they remain competitive and likely face lower-seeded opponents now. Conversely, Eternal Fire's seeding/bracket position is not disclosed. Momentum and opponent matchup quality are unknown. ACADEMY vs FULL-STRENGTH PARITY: Academy teams in esports often field rotating lineups, stand-ins, or players managing multiple rosters. NeverPlay, if running a single focused lineup, may have structural advantage in consistency despite lower ranking. The briefing contains zero injury/availability data for either side. NEVERPLAY RESILIENCE SIGNAL: The fact NeverPlay even advanced past R1 loss (surviving Swiss bracket) suggests they have a functional game plan and teamwork, not a collapsed squad. Their 0-2 loss to INFUR doesn't guarantee weakness vs. Eternal Fire Academy; opponent quality and stylistic matchup matter enormously. RANK GAP OVERSTATED: Valve Global Ranking #68 for Eternal Fire is org-wide; Academy designation almost always implies lower-ranking players. Ranking differential may be far smaller than briefing suggests, especially in a C-Tier online tournament with high variance. MISSING RECENT FORM DATA: Briefing shows NeverPlay 0-2 loss (Apr 7) but NO data on their map pool, veto patterns, individual player form in past 7 days, or Eternal Fire Academy's recent scrim/match record. This asymmetry prevents confident YES scoring. VOLUME AND LATE REVERSAL: Only $5,992 traded; sharp +5.0pp move for NO at 08:00 UTC and -5.5pp for YES at 10:12 UTC on match day itself suggests new information (roster announcement, lineup leak, odds bot recalibration) is shifting market. This late-stage shift toward NO deserves respect in a high-variance academy match. ONLINE FORMAT + TIMEZONE: European online match; technical/connectivity issues and roster fatigue from other events are subtle but real for academy squads. Briefing is silent on whether either team has LAN commitments or scheduling conflicts (we know 3DMAX.A's opponent forfeited due to conflict on same date). CONSENSUS YES (60%) LIKELY ANCHORED ON RANK ALONE: The case for YES relies heavily on Valve ranking and NeverPlay's R1 loss. Neither factor accounts for Academy squad volatility, Swiss bracket dynamics, or real-time roster decisions made hours before match start. Confidence in NO remains elevated because these gaps are real.", "key_factor": "Eternal Fire Academy fielding an unexpectedly depleted or substitute-heavy lineup due to main roster commitments elsewhere, while NeverPlay enters with full-strength motivated squad seeking to avoid elimination in Swiss bracket.", "probability_yes": 0.38, "probability_no": 0.62, "probability_source": "synthetic_mirror"}], "final_score": 0.5555949367088607, "no_score": 0.47447513812154696, "edge": -0.03940506329113924, "no_edge": 0.06947513812154693, "recommendation": "PASS", "confidence_level": "MEDIUM", "signal_type": null, "pass_reason": "Council prob too low: 56% for YES (need \u226557%); YES-side confidence too low: 56% (need \u226567%)", "expected_roi": 0.0, "briefing": {"market": {"id": "1964127", "question": "Counter-Strike: Eternal Fire Academy vs NeverPlay (BO3) - Exort Series Contenders Stage", "resolution_criteria": "This market refers to the Counter-Strike Round 4 match between Eternal Fire Academy and NeverPlay in the Exort Series Contenders Stage, initially scheduled for April 13 at 11:30AM ET.\n\nThis market will resolve to \"Eternal Fire Academy\" if Eternal Fire Academy win the match against NeverPlay.\n\nThis market will resolve to \"NeverPlay\" if NeverPlay win the match against Eternal Fire Academy.\n\nIf the match is canceled (not played at all), ends in a tie, or is delayed beyond 7 days from the scheduled date without a winner determined, this market will resolve to 50-50. \n\nIf the match begins but is not completed, and one team wins due to the opponent's forfeiture, disqualification, or walkover, this market will resolve to the team who wins. \n\nIf the match ends in a forfeit, disqualification, or walkover (team withdraws before the start and the other wins automatically), this market will resolve to 50-50. \n\nThe resolution source for this market will be official information from https://hltv.org. However, if https://hltv.org has not published final results within 2 hours after the event\u2019s conclusion, a consensus of credible reporting may be used instead including video evidence.\n\nIn cases where a team\u2019s listed name includes minor discrepancies from the resolution source, this market will resolve based on the underlying real-world match rather than exact name matching. Recognizable abbreviations, alternate or erroneous spellings, sponsor tags, affiliate or academy designations, regional identifiers, and minor formatting differences will be treated as referring to the same team, provided the intended team can be clearly and uniquely identified within the relevant competition. If a listed team name has no reasonable connection to any participating team, or if it matches or could reasonably refer to another team in the same competition such that the intended team cannot be unambiguously determined, this market will resolve 50-50.", "end_date": "2026-04-13T21:30:00Z", "event_start_time": "2026-04-13T15:30:00Z", "yes_price": 0.595, "volume_usd": 5991.735966000003, "category": "counter-strike", "clob_token_ids": ["21560932918180481023981289999848884093755505766429467313140166032332363375345", "53151276850815591559581217112338024846549857811247038992742965773324260360455"]}, "key_facts": ["Contenders Stage: 16 Team Swiss System Format.", "All matches are Bo3; Top 8 teams proceed to the Main Stage.", "Click here for a detailed explanation of the", "Exort Series #25 is an online European CS2 tournament organized by Exort.", "This C-Tier tournament takes place from Apr 06 to 26 2026 featuring 28 teams", "| Date | Matches |  |\n --- \n| European Pro League Series 6 | | |\n --- \n| 11/04/2026 | MetizportMetizport  2:0  Basement BobsBasement Bobs | Stats |\n|\n| CCT Season 3 Europe Series 20 - 20-22nd | | |\n --- \n| 04/04/2026 | MetizportMetizport  1:2  BebopBebop | Stats |\n| 02/04/2026 | MetizportMetizport  0:2  QWENTRYQWENTRY | Stats |\n| 01/04/2026 | MetizportMetizport  2:0  ENCE AcademyENCE Academy | Stats |\n| 29/03/2026 | MetizportMetizport  1:2  RUSTECRUSTECRUSTEC | Stats |\n|\n| ROG JOURNEY Spring 2026 - 5-8th | | |\n --- \n| 27/03/