evated relative to available evidence Aurora has tangible credentials: XANTARES (veteran Turkish pro), S-tier Esports World Cup 2025 participation, Skyesports Grand Slam 2024 win However, Aurora's 11th place finish at 2025 Esports World Cup indicates inconsistency vs top-tier opponents \u2014 mixed signal on dominance Critical data gap: HOTU roster composition, recent form, and head-to-head record entirely absent from briefing \u2014 cannot properly assess NO side Market pricing at 78% YES appears to over-weight Aurora's pedigree while under-pricing HOTU due to information asymmetry In BO3 format, consistency matters; single tournament wins do not guarantee match victory against unknown but potentially well-prepared opponent Event is TODAY (16 hours to resolution) \u2014 extremely limited time for new information to shift market; current price reflects pre-match consensus Lack of specific recent match data, map pool analysis, or head-to-head history prevents high-confidence assessment of either side Market appears rationally calibrated but slightly crowded on YES side; NO side is underpriced due to information deficit rather than logical weakness Confidence in YES estimate (68%) reflects Aurora's demonstrated tier-1 tournament experience; confidence in NO (45%) reflects data vacuum, not weakness of HOTU position", "key_factor": "Rational", "probability_yes": 0.72, "probability_no": 0.28, "probability_source": "synthetic_mirror"}, {"agent_name": "The Domain Specialist", "probability": 0.76, "confidence_yes": 0.72, "confidence_no": 0.35, "rationale": "Aurora Gaming demonstrably competes at S-tier international tournaments (EWC 2025, Skyesports Grand Slam 2024 winner) \u2014 institutional evidence of ability to perform against elite rosters. XANTARES (age 30, Turkish veteran) is a documented, experienced mid-tier professional player with international tournament exposure \u2014 provides in-game leadership and map knowledge in BO3 format. BO3 format (not BO1) favors consistency and team coordination; Aurora's tournament history suggests structured team play; insufficient data on HOTU's tactical discipline or map pool strategy. Market pricing at 78% YES suggests informed esports betting community has assessed Aurora as clear favorite \u2014 stable trend (\u22123pp over 7 days) indicates consensus rather than volatility-driven speculation. HOTU roster composition, recent form, head-to-head record, and map pool preferences entirely absent from briefing \u2014 represents catastrophic data gap for NO evaluation. IEM Rio Group B stage is high-variance competitive environment; however, Aurora's participation history in prior S-tier events (EWC 11th place 2025) confirms they are invited/seeded, not wildcards. Briefing contains noise (unrelated Astralis, Jame, Eyeballers references, timeline data) that reduces signal-to-noise ratio but does not contradict Aurora > HOTU assessment. No contradictory evidence (injury, roster changes, recent upsets by HOTU) exists in briefing to challenge market consensus. Confidence_no deliberately suppressed to 35 due to asymmetric information burden: briefing provides concrete Aurora data but zero HOTU-specific intelligence, violating two-sided evaluation premise.", "key_factor": "Aurora Gaming's competitive pedigree at S-tier events (Esports World Cup 2025, Skyesports Grand Slam 2024) and XANTARES' veteran leadership provide measurable structural advantage, but HOTU's complete absence from briefing data creates severe information asymmetry that systematically underweights NO side confidence.", "probability_yes": 0.76, "probability_no": 0.24, "probability_source": "synthetic_mirror"}, {"agent_name": "The Contrarian", "probability": 0.72, "confidence_yes": 0.68, "confidence_no": 0.45, "rationale": "Market consensus sits at 78% YES, a 6pp premium above my 72% estimate \u2014 suggests mild overvaluation of Aurora's veteran experience without recent head-to-head data. Aurora's S-tier credentials (EWC 2025 11th place, Skyesports 2024 win) are real but mixed: 11th at elite level is mid-tier performance, not dominant. HOTU roster is almost entirely absent from briefing \u2014 NO side data quality is severely degraded. Confidence_no capped at 45 due to unknown underdog strength, recent form, and map preferences. Briefing is corrupted/sparse: contains irrelevant timeline data (PUBG, Fortnite, Chess), garbled tournament listings, and no HOTU competitive history. This information void is precisely where black swans hide. BO3 format amplifies tactical surprise: if HOTU has a specialized map pool or anti-XANTARES mid-round system, Aurora's individual veteran skill cannot carry through 3 maps without adaptive coaching. IEM Rio is known for upsets in early bracket stages; Group B implies seeding uncertainty. Market may be anchoring on 'XANTARES name recognition' rather than current form against unfamiliar opponent. No recent match data between these teams: Aurora may have built reputation against CIS-region teams (GLuck tournaments listed), while HOTU's region/scrim results are hidden. Absence of evidence is not evidence of absence: HOTU's invisibility in briefing could indicate either weak team OR deliberately low-profile preparation avoiding public demos and scout footage. Aurora's corporate transition (new Head of Esports 2025-06-21, new CS2 Manager 2025-04-05) suggests mid-2025 roster flux; current lineup cohesion vs BO3 stamina unknown. 78% YES pricing implies ~4.3:1 odds; typical BO3 variance is \u00b15-8pp for teams of similar tier. Without strong HOTU evidence, 72% reflects cautious discount for uncertainty, not conviction. Confidence asymmetry justified: Aurora's tournament history is documented (high confidence_yes); HOTU is a void (low confidence_no). Council should flag HOTU intel gap as critical blind spot.", "key_factor": "HOTU executes a surprise tactical meta (likely mid-round economy exploit or map-specific anti-strat) that Aurora's experienced core cannot adapt to across a BO3 format, exploiting roster composition gaps not captured in brief.", "probability_yes": 0.72, "probability_no": 0.28, "probability_source": "synthetic_mirror"}], "final_score": 0.6895444685466378, "no_score": 0.3213698630136987, "edge": -0.09545553145336227, "no_edge": 0.10636986301369872, "recommendation": "PASS", "confidence_level": "MEDIUM", "signal_type": null, "pass_reason": "YES-side confidence too low: 66% (need \u226567%)", "expected_roi": 0.0, "briefing": {"market": {"id": "1842278", "question": "Counter-Strike: Aurora Gaming vs HOTU (BO3) - IEM Rio Group B", "resolution_criteria": "This market refers to the Counter-Strike Upper bracket quarterfinal 3 match between Aurora Gaming and HOTU in the IEM Rio Group B, initially scheduled for April 13 at 10:00AM ET.\n\nThis market will resolve to \"Aurora Gaming\" if Aurora Gaming win the match against HOTU.\n\nThis market will resolve to \"HOTU\" if HOTU win the match against Aurora Gaming.\n\nIf the match is canceled (not played at all), ends in a tie, or is delayed beyond 7 days from the scheduled date without a winner determined, this market will resolve to 50-50. \n\nIf the match begins but is not completed, and one team wins due to the opponent's forfeiture, disqualification, or walkover, this market will resolve to the team who wins. \n\nIf the match ends in a forfeit, disqualification, or walkover (team withdraws before the start and the other wins automatically), this market will resolve to 50-50. \n\nThe resolution source for this market will be official information from https://hltv.org. However, if https://hltv.org has not published final results within 2 hours after the event\u2019s conclusion, a consensus of credible reporting may be used instead including video evidence.\n\nIn cases where a team\u2019s listed name includes minor discrepancies from the resolution source, this market will resolve based on the underlying real-world match rather than exact name matching. Recognizable abbrevia