onsensus (1.62 vs 2.25 odds) suggest Navi remains the slightly stronger technical unit.", "probability_yes": 0.58, "probability_no": 0.42000000000000004, "probability_source": "synthetic_mirror"}, {"agent_name": "The Contrarian", "probability": 0.52, "confidence_yes": 0.42, "confidence_no": 0.38, "rationale": "BRIEFING DATA GAPS: No recent form data, head-to-head records, individual player performance metrics, or map pool analysis provided\u2014this creates asymmetric confidence on both sides. HOME CROWD WILDCARD: FURIA plays in Brazil; Navi travels to unfamiliar territory. Day-1 favorites won, but that sample (8/8) is small and may not account for home-venue psychological uplift in a BO3 where momentum swings are critical. ORG FRICTION SIGNAL: Briefing explicitly notes community dislike of Navi org ('i do not like navi org') and hints at internal cohesion concerns. In BO3 format, mental fatigue and trust breakdowns compound faster than in single maps. JERSEY CONTROVERSY AS DISTRACTION PROXY: The yellow jersey 'copying Vitality' controversy and rapid ticket sellout suggest FURIA has strong local fanbase energy and media attention\u2014psychological tailwind for underdogs in home tournaments. ODDSMAKER CONSENSUS TRAP: Navi favored 1.62 vs 2.25, but market prices show stable 56/44 split with minimal volatility. This suggests quiet disagreement: smart money may be pricing in FURIA's home advantage, not just raw roster strength. KYOUSUKE HYPE SKEPTICISM: Community questions ('I don't understand all the hype around kyousuke') suggest at least one key Navi player may be overrated or underperforming\u2014a structural weakness masked by aggregate odds. BO3 FORMAT FAVORS ADAPTABILITY: Best-of-3 rewards tactical flexibility and momentum. FURIA's underdog status may correlate with creative map bans/picks; Navi's 'stability' may translate to predictability. DAY-1 SAMPLE SIZE CAVEAT: 8/8 favorites winning is small-sample noise. Tournament momentum rarely persists linearly; underdogs often exploit fatigue and tactical reads on day 2+. MISSING VETO DATA: No map pool, veto strategy, or recent map-specific records provided. If FURIA has a favored map pool advantage or Navi has known weaknesses, this shifts odds significantly. CONFIDENCE ASYMMETRY RATIONALE: confidence_yes set lower (42) because 'roster stability' and 'org perception' are subjective; confidence_no set lower (38) because home-crowd advantage alone is insufficient without form data. Both sides lack quantitative ground truth.", "key_factor": "FURIA's home-crowd momentum and tactical familiarity with Brazilian LAN conditions overwhelms Navi's roster stability, particularly if Navi's rumored internal org friction manifests as coordination lapses under high-pressure BO3 format.", "probability_yes": 0.52, "probability_no": 0.48, "probability_source": "synthetic_mirror"}], "final_score": 0.5534101382488479, "no_score": 0.46414565826330534, "edge": -0.0015898617511521662, "no_edge": 0.019145658263305387, "recommendation": "PASS", "confidence_level": "MEDIUM", "signal_type": null, "pass_reason": "Council prob too low: 55% for YES (need \u226557%); YES-side confidence too low: 62% (need \u226567%)", "expected_roi": 0.0, "briefing": {"market": {"id": "1974869", "question": "Counter-Strike: Natus Vincere vs FURIA (BO3) - IEM Rio Group B", "resolution_criteria": "This market refers to the Counter-Strike Upper bracket semifinal 1 match between Natus Vincere and FURIA in the IEM Rio Group B, initially scheduled for April 14 at 2:00PM ET.\n\nThis market will resolve to \"Natus Vincere\" if Natus Vincere win the match against FURIA.\n\nThis market will resolve to \"FURIA\" if FURIA win the match against Natus Vincere.\n\nIf the match is canceled (not played at all), ends in a tie, or is delayed beyond 7 days from the scheduled date without a winner determined, this market will resolve to 50-50. \n\nIf the match begins but is not completed, and one team wins due to the opponent's forfeiture, disq