{"run_id": "fdf67975-198c-4e16-af8f-83a9e573ccf3", "timestamp": "2026-04-16T14:32:26.859210+00:00", "legacy": false, "result": {"market": {"id": "1997381", "question": "Counter-Strike: PURE vs ENRAGE (BO1) - ESEA Advanced Europe Regular Season", "resolution_criteria": "This market refers to the Counter-Strike match between PURE and ENRAGE in the ESEA Advanced Europe Regular Season, initially scheduled for April 16 at 1:00PM ET.\n\nThis market will resolve to \"PURE\" if PURE win the match against ENRAGE.\n\nThis market will resolve to \"ENRAGE\" if ENRAGE win the match against PURE.\n\nIf the match is canceled (not played at all), ends in a tie, or is delayed beyond 7 days from the scheduled date without a winner determined, this market will resolve to 50-50. \n\nIf the match begins but is not completed, and one team wins due to the opponent's forfeiture, disqualification, or walkover, this market will resolve to the team who wins. \n\nIf the match ends in a forfeit, disqualification, or walkover (team withdraws before the start and the other wins automatically), this market will resolve to 50-50. \n\nThe resolution source for this market will be official information from https://hltv.org. However, if https://hltv.org has not published final results within 2 hours after the event\u2019s conclusion, a consensus of credible reporting may be used instead including video evidence.\n\nIn cases where a team\u2019s listed name includes minor discrepancies from the resolution source, this market will resolve based on the underlying real-world match rather than exact name matching. Recognizable abbreviations, alternate or erroneous spellings, sponsor tags, affiliate or academy designations, regional identifiers, and minor formatting differences will be treated as referring to the same team, provided the intended team can be clearly and uniquely identified within the relevant competition. If a listed team name has no reasonable connection to any participating team, or if it matches or could reasonably refer to another team in the same competition such that the intended team cannot be unambiguously determined, this market will resolve 50-50.", "end_date": "2026-04-16T23:00:00Z", "event_start_time": "2026-04-16T17:00:00Z", "yes_price": 0.45, "volume_usd": 0.0, "category": "counter-strike", "clob_token_ids": ["112897994142012484622070851780721664745433814331317995468503366907646924671241", "4386115839876554901742750878195507391418802274080959802991041187031945378946"], "sports_market_type": "moneyline"}, "verdicts": [{"agent_name": "The Rules Lawyer", "probability": 0.45, "probability_yes": 0.45, "probability_no": 0.55, "probability_source": "independent", "confidence_yes": 0.18, "confidence_no": 0.2, "rationale": "RESOLUTION CONDITION: Market resolves to PURE if PURE wins the BO1 match; resolves to ENRAGE if ENRAGE wins. Outcome is binary (no draws in BO1) unless match is canceled, delayed >7 days without result, or ends in forfeit/disqualification (which trigger 50-50). DATA AVAILABILITY: Briefing contains only Liquipedia structural information from ESEA Season 54 Advanced Europe; no roster details, recent performance stats, win rates, map pool compatibility, player skill differential, or prior head-to-head results between PURE and ENRAGE. MARKET PRICING CONTEXT: Current YES=45%, NO=55% with zero USD volume. Price movement shows volatility (\u00b15-5.5pp in past ~2 hours) but reflects orderbook positioning, not fundamental team analysis. Lack of volume and data suggests prices are noise rather than consensus intelligence. TEMPORAL COHERENCE: Event starts in ~2.5 hours (17:00 UTC on 2026-04-16). All historical ESEA season data in briefing (S36, S43, S53, S57) are prior encounters; none apply directly to this Season 54 match. Insufficient time for major information arrival; outcome will largely depend on team preparation, roster stability, and in-match execution. CONFIDENCE ASYMMETRY: confidence_yes=18 and confidence_no=20 both reflect severe data scarcity. Neither side has credible supporting evidence. The small 5pp spread (45 vs 55) is within noise margin and likely driven by orderbook microstructure rather than predictive signal. DUAL-PERSPECTIVE ASSESSMENT: YES-side evidence (probability_score=45) is non-existent; PURE's capabilities are unknown. NO-side evidence (probability_no_score=55) is equally absent; ENRAGE's strengths/weaknesses are unknown. Market prices suggest slight NO lean, but this is uninformative given total absence of team data. FORFEIT/CANCELLATION RISK: Resolution criteria specify 50-50 for cancellations, >7-day delays without result, or pre-match forfeits. No indicators in briefing suggest elevated cancellation risk, but with imminent start and zero operational data, this tail risk (estimated 2-5%) is non-negligible and reduces confidence on both sides. RESOLUTION MECHANISM: HLTV.org is resolution source; fallback to credible reporting if HLTV unpublished within 2 hours post-event. This is low-risk; HLTV reliably covers ESEA matches within 2 hours. Mechanism risk is minimal (\u22643pp impact on confidence). CONCLUSION: In absence of any substantive team information, the 45/55 market split is best treated as reflecting genuine uncertainty with a slight NO tilt driven by orderbook supply/demand. Assign probability_score=45 and probability_no_score=55 to match market consensus, but apply very low confidence on both sides (18 and 20) to reflect data vacuum. No technical resolution risk identified; main risk is epistemic.", "key_factor": "No team roster, recent form, head-to-head record, or competitive data available for either PURE or ENRAGE; current market prices (45/55) reflect pure orderbook mechanics with minimal informational content; imminent event timing means reversal requires significant real-time intelligence."}, {"agent_name": "The Bull", "probability": 0.55, "probability_yes": 0.55, "probability_no": 0.45, "probability_source": "independent", "confidence_yes": 0.25, "confidence_no": 0.25, "rationale": "CRITICAL DATA GAP: The briefing contains NO roster information, NO recent form data, NO head-to-head history, and NO performance metrics for either PURE or ENRAGE in ESEA S54. The Liquipedia data is structural noise (unrelated seasons, team names without context). Market pricing (NO at 55%, YES at 45%) reflects a marginal favorite, not a strong consensus. This thin edge suggests the market itself is operating under severe information constraints. Recent price action shows SHARP VOLATILITY: Apr 16 13:00 UTC saw a -5.5pp swing on YES (toward NO), followed by a +5.0pp and -5.0pp whipsaw on the NO side. These movements are consistent with thin liquidity ($0 volume reported) and noise rather than informed trading. LIVE/IMMINENT TIMING (2.5 hours to event start) means reversal catalysts (roster changes, forfeit announcements, injury news) are INCREASINGLY UNLIKELY. If substantial new information existed, it would have moved the market already. BOTH SIDES ARE UNDERCONFIDENT: Because neither team has evaluable recent-season data in the briefing, confidence in BOTH probability_score and probability_no_score must be capped at ~25. This is NOT a coin flip (50-50); it's a data-starved market where the price IS the signal. FAVOR NO because market pricing at 55% for the underdog (PURE at 45%) suggests informed traders may have detected structural advantage in ENRAGE (roster stability, head-to-head edge, map pool fit) not visible in the briefing. The market's 10pp edge for NO is small but consistent with rational edge in BO1 variance. CAVEAT: Absence of evidence is NOT evidence of absence. PURE may be a strong team that simply lacks public Liquipedia coverage. Without data on EITHER side, the 55-45 split is as defensible as 45-55. CONFIDENCE ASYMMETRY: confidence_no (25) = confidence_yes (25) because the briefing provides NO differential support for either team. We advocate for NO based on market price authority in an information vacuum, not on substantive team analysis.", "key_factor": "Market equilibrium at 55-45 split reflects genuine uncertainty; NO is favored only by pricing inertia in absence of actionable team data."}, {"agent_name": "The Bear", "probability": 0.45, "probability_yes": 0.45, "probability_no": 0.55, "probability_source": "independent", "confidence_yes": 0.2, "confidence_no": 0.22, "rationale": "CRITICAL DATA GAP: The briefing contains zero substantive information about either team's current roster, recent form, head-to-head record, or performance metrics in ESEA Season 54. Both teams are essentially opaque. Market pricing (YES 45%, NO 55%) appears to reflect NO as slight favorite, but without performance data, this pricing cannot be justified on merit. It may reflect liquidity imbalances, arbitrary backer preference, or historical market convention rather than skill assessment. For an underdog advocacy: PURE being priced 10 percentage points lower (45% vs 55%) despite zero differentiating evidence suggests potential mispricing. In BO1 matches with information vacuums, upset probability is often higher than markets price because single-map variance is high and team matchup specifics matter enormously. BO1 format inherently favors variance over deterministic outcomes. A 10-point spread based on no data is suspicious \u2014 suggests the market is pricing on meta-signals (team reputation, historical league position, sponsor tier) rather than Season 54 performance. Price action history shows a sharp 5.5pp drop for YES at Apr 16 13:00 UTC, followed by NO volatility (+5.0pp, then -5.0pp). This noise pattern and minimal volume ($0 USD reported) suggest low liquidity and possible algorithmic/automated positioning rather than informed betting. No head-to-head data, no recent match records, no roster changes reported for either team \u2014 this is a blind match by the briefing standard. Under such conditions, assuming either side has >60% win probability is overconfident. ENRAGE (NO side) has no documented strengths in the briefing, only the default favorite status. No roster stability, form trend, or tournament performance is provided. The favorite's case is as hollow as the underdog's. Upside for YES (PURE): In information-starved BO1 matches, the underdog often captures value because single-map play permits surprise map pick or team chemistry to outweigh perceived skill gap. If PURE has even modest recent form momentum (not documented in briefing but plausible given silence), they are underpriced. Risk for YES: ENRAGE's +10pp pricing advantage, however unjustified by data, may reflect genuine market knowledge (team reputation, sponsorship tier, or recent performance) not captured in this briefing. Confidence must remain low. FINAL ASSESSMENT: With zero performance data for either side, the market's 45%/55% split is noise rather than signal. PURE at 45% may be mildly undervalued, but without concrete evidence of their form, confidence in an upset must remain below 25. NO at 55% is equally unsupported, so confidence there is also capped at ~22. This is essentially a coin flip with arbitrary price anchoring.", "key_factor": "Complete absence of concrete team performance data means market pricing reflects no informational advantage and may be driven by arbitrary positioning rather than skill differential."}, {"agent_name": "The Base-Rate Statistician", "probability": 0.45, "probability_yes": 0.45, "probability_no": 0.55, "probability_source": "independent", "confidence_yes": 0.25, "confidence_no": 0.25, "rationale": "CRITICAL DATA GAP: Briefing contains zero actionable information about PURE or ENRAGE \u2014 no roster composition, recent form, head-to-head history, or current-season standing. Search results are structural wiki fragments from ESEA S54 (team lists, rule references) and historical data from non-contiguous seasons (S36, S43, S57), none of which identify or characterize PURE or ENRAGE. Without team performance history, individual player strength, map pool compatibility, or recent match results, no principled base rate can be established beyond 'coin flip' for a BO1 between unknown opponents. Current market pricing (45% YES / 55% NO) suggests modest NO-side edge, but this could reflect: (i) genuine edge data from bettors with roster knowledge unavailable in briefing, (ii) random walk / bid-ask spread, or (iii) late algorithmic rebalancing. EVENT TIMING (LIVE/IMMINENT, ~2.5 hrs to start): At this stage, any probability shift requires significant late news (forfeit notice, roster scandal, etc.). No such news present in briefing. BO1 format adds variance \u2014 single-map matches are inherently closer to 50-50 than BO3/BO5, even for disparate teams. Confidence severely constrained by complete absence of team-level data. Both sides equally under-evidenced in briefing. Market pricing slightly favors NO (55%), but gap is narrow and may be noise given zero volume ($0 USD traded). ASSIGNMENT: Defer to market pricing (45/55) as a weak signal, but flag that this assignment carries very low epistemic confidence on both sides.", "key_factor": "Counter-Strike BO1 matches: ~50% win rate for any given team in a competitive league setting absent roster/form data; no reliable base rate available for team-specific outcomes in ESEA Advanced Europe."}, {"agent_name": "The Sentiment Analyst", "probability": 0.45, "probability_yes": 0.45, "probability_no": 0.55, "probability_source": "independent", "confidence_yes": 0.18, "confidence_no": 0.19, "rationale": "CRITICAL DATA GAP: Briefing contains zero team-specific intelligence on PURE or ENRAGE \u2014 no roster info, recent form, head-to-head history, map pool, or season standings. Market price (45% YES / 55% NO) appears mechanically balanced near 50-50, suggesting traders are pricing genuine ignorance rather than informed conviction. Price history shows recent sharp moves (\u00b15.5pp on Apr 16 13:00 UTC), indicating some trading activity, but no substantive news catalyst tied to those moves in the briefing. With ~2.5 hours until match start (LIVE/IMMINENT), any major signal (roster issues, map bans, team news) would likely have leaked by now. Absence of such signals in briefing suggests market has no access to insider information. Liquipedia dump in briefing references S53 and other historical seasons, not current season 54 performance data. Suggests briefing was harvested before S54 advanced stats became available. BO1 format favors volatility and upsets; without team-specific data, neither 45% nor 55% can be justified as anything but a coin-flip proxy. Extremely low confidence on BOTH sides reflects genuine data asymmetry \u2014 briefing provides structural context but zero predictive power on match outcome. Market is rationally apathetic because the briefing itself is apathetic. Crowd is not exuberant or panicked; it is blank. Current prices (YES 45%, NO 55%) may reflect slight underdog lean toward ENRAGE, but without team form data, this could be random orderbook friction or a guess with no evidentiary support in briefing. Confidence scores kept very low (18, 19) to reflect that any probability assigned here is largely noise given the complete absence of match-relevant intelligence in the briefing.", "key_factor": "Apathy"}, {"agent_name": "The Domain Specialist", "probability": 0.45, "probability_yes": 0.45, "probability_no": 0.55, "probability_source": "independent", "confidence_yes": 0.15, "confidence_no": 0.15, "rationale": "CRITICAL DATA GAP: Briefing contains zero actionable intel on PURE or ENRAGE\u2014no current roster, no S54 regular-season record, no recent tournament results, no map preferences, no player skill ratings, and no head-to-head history for this season. Market Price Anchoring Risk: Current YES/NO prices (45%/55%) appear to reflect shallow liquidity ($0 volume) and likely rest on assumption of relative team strength unknown to this analyst. The 10pp NO advantage could be correct or pure noise. BO1 Inherent Variance: Single-map elimination format in CS:GO/CS2 is high-variance even between known teams. One player's clutch round or an eco-round decision can swing the match. This variance is NOT captured by traditional win-probability models. ESEA Advanced Context: This is a regional developmental/semi-pro division, not the professional Pro League. Team stability is lower, roster changes frequent, and performance data older. Teams may not have consistent coaching or practice infrastructure. Temporal Pressure: Event is LIVE/IMMINENT (~2.5 hours until start). No time for new information to emerge. If sharp bettors had strong conviction, price movement would already be visible; the flat 24-hour action (0pp change) suggests genuine uncertainty or low-confidence market participants. Search Result Failure: Liquipedia wiki excerpt contains only redlinks and TBD placeholders for S54. This suggests the season is either very new, the data is not yet populated, or both teams are genuinely obscure/new to the league. Asymmetric Confidence Justification: confidence_yes = 15 and confidence_no = 15 (not summing to 100) reflects genuine epistemic humility. The briefing provides NO differential evidence favoring either side. Any confidence above 25 would be false precision. Market Integrity: The sharp moves visible (Apr 16 13:00 UTC \u00b15pp swings) suggest informed trading, but without context about what triggered them, they cannot be reverse-engineered into skill assessments. Outcome: Assign probability_score = 45 (matching market YES price) and probability_no_score = 55 (matching market NO price) as a conservative reflection that market participants have marginally favored ENRAGE, but with such weak evidence that my confidence in EITHER side is below 20.", "key_factor": "BO1 format in ESEA Advanced creates extreme variance; without current roster data, recent map pool performance, or head-to-head records, any confidence estimate is pure guesswork masked by market price anchoring."}, {"agent_name": "The Contrarian", "probability": 0.42, "probability_yes": 0.42, "probability_no": 0.58, "probability_source": "independent", "confidence_yes": 0.18, "confidence_no": 0.22, "rationale": "BRIEFING CONTAINS ZERO ACTIONABLE DATA: No team rosters, no recent match results, no current standings, no head-to-head history, no map picks, no individual player stats or form trajectories. The entire briefing is structural scaffolding (ESEA season metadata, historical S53/S57 references) with no competitive signal. MARKET PRICES (45/55) SUGGEST NEAR-PARITY: The 10-point spread (NO favored) is shallow and sits atop zero concrete evidence. This screams consensus uncertainty rather than informed edge\u2014a hallmark of mispriceable markets. RECENT SHARP MOVE (Apr 16 13:00 UTC, -5.5pp YES / +5.0pp NO) OCCURRED ~4 HOURS BEFORE MATCH START: No explanatory news in briefing. Possible triggers: roster announcement, injury leak, scrim result, or simply whale positioning. The absence of publicly-linked cause increases probability of *informational asymmetry*\u2014some traders know something not yet in the briefing. BO1 FORMAT AMPLIFIES VARIANCE: Single map eliminates best-of series regression to mean. A team's pick/veto skill, map pool depth mismatch, or tactical surprise (meta shift, anti-strat innovation) has outsized impact. PURE or ENRAGE could possess an undocumented strategic edge. BLACK SWAN #1 \u2014 ROSTER FLUIDITY IN TIER-2/3 EUROPEAN CS: ESEA Advanced is developmental tier. Teams routinely cycle stand-ins for unavailability (visa, last-minute work conflicts, illness). If PURE lost a core player (IGL, entry, lurker) to forced substitution hours before kickoff, a prepared academy stand-in could crater PURE's default execution. Inverse: an ENRAGE stand-in could bottleneck them. BLACK SWAN #2 \u2014 META/PATCH INNOVATION: Counter-Strike undergoes sub-weekly tactical shifts. A fresh utility meta, weapon economy tweak, or map-specific read discovered in recent scrims could favor one team's playstyle. ENRAGE may have cracked an anti-PURE default; PURE may have a new aggro-read advantage. Briefing silence on tactics is data itself. BLACK SWAN #3 \u2014 MORALE/MOTIVATION ASYMMETRY: Invisible before match start. If PURE has already secured playoff seeding and ENRAGE is fighting for qualification (or vice versa), mental pressure compounds variance. Burned-out favorites collapse in BO1s; hungrier underdogs clutch. CONFIDENCE ASYMMETRY JUSTIFIED: confidence_yes (18) and confidence_no (22) both LOW because briefing provides zero empirical footing for either side. The market is pricing on player intuition or opaque information leakage, not public data. A 4-point confidence gap (confidence_no > confidence_yes) reflects that NO's 10-point price premium suggests *some* signal reached traders\u2014but that signal is not visible here. PROBABILITY CALIBRATION: Absent concrete data, default to market midpoint as anchor (47.5%), then apply modest downward pressure to YES (42) to account for the recent -5.5pp dump at 13:00 UTC, which suggests late smart money favored ENRAGE. This is weak evidence, but it is *the only* evidence in the briefing. IF THIS MATCH RESOLVES TONIGHT, EXPECT SHARP REVERSAL: Once the match outcome is known, shares will trade to $0.01/$0.99 in the 2\u20134 hours before official HLTV confirmation. Watch live score for which direction the gap widens; that will retroactively validate whether the briefing's silence masked PURE or ENRAGE strength.", "key_factor": "PURE's primary IGL or fragging anchor suffers last-minute illness/injury forcing an emergency stand-in with minimal preparation time, or ENRAGE executes a recently-discovered meta-read on the map pool that neutralizes PURE's default setups."}], "final_score": 0.463145390967616, "no_score": 0.5385109482622958, "edge": 0.23518518518518522, "no_edge": 0.13518518518518519, "recommendation": "PASS", "confidence_level": "LOW", "signal_type": null, "pass_reason": "Council prob too low: 54% for NO (need \u226557%); NO-side confidence too low: 21% (need \u226567%)", "expected_roi": 0.0, "perceived_win_rate_at_run": 0.6851851851851852, "kelly_stake_full": 0.0, "kelly_stake_scaled": 0.0, "kelly_multiplier": 0.0, "agent_weights_snapshot": {"The Rules Lawyer": 1.0702687976791527, "The Bull": 0.9559930478535308, "The Base-Rate Statistician": 1.0562331438919461, "The Sentiment Analyst": 1.0626106949123544, "The Domain Specialist": 1.059634504436164, "The Contrarian": 1.0571973090855826, "The Bear": 0.73806250214127}, "briefing": {"market": {"id": "1997381", "question": "Counter-Strike: PURE vs ENRAGE (BO1) - ESEA Advanced Europe Regular Season", "resolution_criteria": "This market refers to the Counter-Strike match between PURE and ENRAGE in the ESEA Advanced Europe Regular Season, initially scheduled for April 16 at 1:00PM ET.\n\nThis market will resolve to \"PURE\" if PURE win the match against ENRAGE.\n\nThis market will resolve to \"ENRAGE\" if ENRAGE win the match against PURE.\n\nIf the match is canceled (not played at all), ends in a tie, or is delayed beyond 7 days from the scheduled date without a winner determined, this market will resolve to 50-50. \n\nIf the match begins but is not completed, and one team wins due to the opponent's forfeiture, disqualification, or walkover, this market will resolve to the team who wins. \n\nIf the match ends in a forfeit, disqualification, or walkover (team withdraws before the start and the other wins automatically), this market will resolve to 50-50. \n\nThe resolution source for this market will be official information from https://hltv.org. However, if https://hltv.org has not published final results within 2 hours after the event\u2019s conclusion, a consensus of credible reporting may be used instead including video evidence.\n\nIn cases where a team\u2019s listed name includes minor discrepancies from the resolution source, this market will resolve based on the underlying real-world match rather than exact 