 reasonable connection to any participating team, or if it matches or could reasonably refer to another team in the same competition such that the intended team cannot be unambiguously determined, this market will resolve 50-50.", "end_date": "2026-04-17T00:00:00Z", "event_start_time": "2026-04-16T18:00:00Z", "yes_price": 0.37, "volume_usd": 3583.2260000000006, "category": "league-of-legends", "clob_token_ids": ["61505405503674775624491915475347734554775768354383148241293440767128960807134", "63363176793874946752579533728440884940445477267028089802874402954607800095816"], "sports_market_type": "moneyline"}, "verdicts": [{"agent_name": "The Rules Lawyer", "probability": 0.38, "probability_yes": 0.38, "probability_no": 0.62, "probability_source": "independent", "confidence_yes": 0.62, "confidence_no": 0.68, "rationale": "TEMPORAL COHERENCE CONFIRMED: Event starts 2026-04-16 18:00 UTC (~3 hours from briefing time). All scorelines in case narratives (1-0, 1-3, 3-0) describe PRIOR Winter 2026 playoffs encounters, NOT this market. This market's outcome is still undetermined. HEAD-TO-HEAD CONTEXT (Winter 2026 Playoffs): BIG holds a 2-1 advantage over Spandau in recent playoffs. BIG won the series 3-1 and 2-3 (likely BO5 format), while Spandau won Game 1 of the semifinals 1-0 but lost the series overall. In BO1 format, this history is informative but not determinative. CASE FOR YES (Spandau): (a) Spandau demonstrated BO1 capability, winning Game 1 of Winter semifinals 1-0 vs BIG; (b) Multiple 1-0 wins in Winter regular season (vs TOG, ROSS) show decisiveness and map control in single games; (c) BO1 format removes series disadvantage\u2014Spandau's ability to win individual maps is proven. CASE FOR NO (BIG): (a) BIG won 2 of 3 documented playoff encounters vs Spandau (series outcomes 3-1 and 2-3); (b) Superior macro play and kill share (27 kills, 68.6% share) in one playoff match demonstrates gameplay edge; (c) Consistency across multiple matchups suggests structural advantage. BO1 VARIANCE FACTOR: Single-game formats are inherently high-variance. Head-to-head series records are less predictive in BO1 than in BO3/BO5. BIG's 2-1 series advantage does not linearly translate to a 67% BO1 win probability; variance reduces predictability. This supports a probability closer to 50% than the 2-1 ratio alone would suggest. ROSTER & META RISK: Winter playoffs (March 5, 2026) to Spring regular season (April 16, 2026) represents a ~6-week gap. Both teams may have roster changes, meta shifts, or preparation adjustments. Briefing mentions historical roster churn on Spandau but does not provide current Spring 2026 roster confirmation. This asymmetric uncertainty slightly favors the market price (YES 37%, NO 63%) but warrants moderate, not high, confidence. MARKET PRICE CALIBRATION: Market prices YES at 37% / NO at 63%. This aligns with BIG's 2-1 playoff head-to-head advantage, adjusted downward for BO1 variance. The price appears reasonable given available data, though the gap (26 percentage points) may slightly overweight BIG's series advantage relative to BO1 unpredictability. CONFIDENCE ASYMMETRY: Confidence_yes = 62 (BIG's recent dominance and consistent macro play are well-documented); confidence_no = 68 (BIG's advantages are clearer, but roster/meta changes between Winter and Spring introduce residual uncertainty). Both confidences are moderate due to: (a) BO1 variance inherently limits predictability, (b) roster/meta uncertainty over 6-week gap, (c) single prior BO1 sample (Game 1 of playoffs) is limited. RESOLUTION CRITERIA CLARITY: Market will resolve to the team that wins the BO1 match. No ambiguity in team names (Eintracht Spandau and BIG are both unambiguously identified). Forfeit/disqualification scenarios are possible but low-prior. Resolution source (gol.gg) is standard for Prime League. No technical resolution risk flagged. PROBABILITY ASSIGNMENT: probability_score = 38 (reflecting BIG's 2-1 playoff advantage tempered by BO1 variance; Spandau's demonstrated single-game capability; and mid-range uncertainty from roster/meta changes). probability_no_score = 62 (reflecting BIG's consistent dominance, superior macro play, and series head-to-head edge, but acknowledging BO1 unpredictability prevents higher confidence). Sum = 100 (no residual ignorance mass; briefing provides sufficient data for a dual-sided assessment).", "key_factor": "BO1 format introduces high variance; single-game outcomes are inherently less predictable than series play, and roster changes between Winter playoffs and Spring regular season may shift team dynamics unpredictably."}, {"agent_name": "The Bull", "probability": 0.63, "probability_yes": 0.63, "probability_no": 0.62, "probability_source": "independent", "confidence_yes": 0.42, "confidence_no": 0.58, "rationale": "FAVORED SIDE (NO/BIG) STRENGTHS: BIG won the Winter playoffs matchup decisively\u2014defeating Spandau 1-3 and 3-0 across two playoff encounters, establishing a 2-1 advantage in recent direct competition. The 27 kills and 68.6% kill share stat reveals macro control and teamfighting superiority. This recent, high-stakes validation in a competitive context carries weight. UNDERDOG WEAKNESS (YES/SPANDAU): While Spandau's Week 1-2 regular-season wins vs TOG and ROSS show decisiveness, neither opponent is BIG\u2014a materially stronger team. The single 1-0 playoff win over BIG in Game 1 of the series was negated by two subsequent losses in the same series, suggesting Spandau's one-off success did not reflect overall advantage. BO1 VARIANCE CAVEAT: BO1 format genuinely increases unpredictability vs series play. This is the briefing's single strongest argument favoring Spandau's upset odds. A single game removes the ability for the stronger team to adjust and exploit weaknesses. At 37%, Spandau is receiving fair value from variance alone. SEASONAL TRANSITION UNCERTAINTY: Spring 2026 regular season follows Winter 2026 playoffs. Roster continuity is flagged in the briefing (multiple player changes logged, though exact current rosters are unclear). Meta shifts and new compositions may disrupt prior form. This cuts both ways but introduces genuine noise that moderates BIG's advantage. TEMPORAL CONSTRAINT: Event starts in ~3 hours. No new major catalyst (injury, roster change, public statement) is documented in the briefing to shift momentum before match time. Market pricing at 63% NO reflects rational consensus given available data. HEAD-TO-HEAD WEIGHTING: BIG's 2-1 playoff record vs Spandau is the most recent, highest-stakes context. It overweights Spandau's regular-season dominance vs weaker teams. In direct matchups, BIG proved more versatile. CONFIDENCE ASYMMETRY: Strong confidence in the BIG case (58% on NO side) rests on clear playoff dominance and macro metrics. However, confidence on the Spandau case is lower (42% on YES side) because BO1 variance and roster uncertainty create genuine blind spots. The briefing does not provide current rosters, meta snapshot, or Spring-specific form data\u2014this ignorance mass justifies the lower YES confidence despite the market being 37/63. MARKET COHERENCE: The 63% NO price aligns with BIG's empirical advantage but may slightly undervalue BO1 variance and seasonal uncertainty. A fair probability is likely 60\u201365% NO, making 63% reasonable and possibly fairly priced or marginally favoring NO at the edges.", "key_factor": "BIG's demonstrated dominance in Winter playoffs head-to-head (2-1 series record with superior macro execution) positions them as the stronger team entering Spring regular season."}, {"agent_name": "The Bear", "probability": 0.42, "probability_yes": 0.42, "probability_no": 0.61, "probability_source": "independent", "confidence_yes": 0.35, "confidence_no": 0.62, "rationale": "UNDERDOG CASE FOR YES: Spandau has concrete evidence of beating BIG in BO1 format \u2014 they won 1-0 against BIG in Winter Playoffs Game 1, directly contradicting the narrative that BIG is inevitably superior. BO1 FORMAT AMPLIFIES VARIANCE: Single-game elimination removes series structure; BIG's 3-0 and 1-3 playoff wins do not guarantee dominance in a single map. Spandau's earlier 1-0 against BIG in the same playoff series shows they can execute the game plan when it matters. ROSTER INSTABILITY RED FLAG FOR BOTH: Spandau's roster has undergone significant turnover (Pride, Special, SMILEY, nRated departures; multiple joiners in December 2025 and March 2026). BIG's roster context is absent from briefing, creating asymmetric uncertainty. If BIG also experienced roster disruption, their macro consistency may have degraded. SPRING VS WINTER META SHIFT: Winter 2026 playoffs concluded ~6 weeks ago. Meta shifts, patch changes, and team adaptation during Spring regular season are unspecified in briefing. Spandau may have refined their approach; BIG's playoff dominance may not translate cleanly to Spring conditions. MARKET PRICING ASYMMETRY: YES at 37% reflects BIG's playoff series win (2-1 overall). However, BO1 outcomes are inherently more volatile than series. A 2-1 series advantage does not justify a 63-37 odds gap in a single game, especially with missing roster/meta data. STRENGTH OF BIG'S CASE: BIG's 27 kills and 68.6% kill share in one playoff match, combined with two decisive wins (1-3 loss and 3-0 win) in the Winter Playoffs Semifinals, shows sustained macro control and teamfighting dominance. This is concrete evidence of superior execution under pressure. WEAKNESS IN SPANDAU'S CASE: Spandau's Winter regular season record ('multiple 1-0 wins') is vague and unquantified. Their playoff loss (1-3, then losing game in a partial 2-3 series) shows they could not sustain momentum. One Game 1 win does not erase the series-level gap. ROSTER CONTINUITY UNKNOWN: Briefing lists Spandau roster changes (departures Nov-Dec 2025, March 2026) but provides no BIG roster data. If BIG maintained stability while Spandau shuffled players, BIG's chemistry advantage could be decisive. Conversely, if BIG also changed roster, this unknown favors neither side. DATA GAPS FOR CONFIDENCE ADJUSTMENT: No Spring 2026 match data is present (event occurs TODAY, April 16). No BIG roster, recent scrim form, or Spring meta context exists. High uncertainty on both sides reduces confidence even as BIG remains slight favorite based on Winter playoff precedent. CONCLUSION: BIG is the rational favorite based on Winter playoff series performance (2-1). However, in BO1 format with roster flux and meta uncertainty, Spandau's 1-0 win proof-of-concept and volatile single-game nature justify a modest upward correction to YES. 42% reflects that BO1 variance and missed data support a closer matchup than 37% implies, but BIG's sustained playoff dominance keeps them favored overall.", "key_factor": "BO1 format volatility and Spandau's demonstrated ability to beat BIG in single games (1-0 Winter playoffs Game 1) combined with roster adjustments potentially disrupting BIG's macro dominance."}, {"agent_name": "The Base-Rate Statistician", "probability": 0.38, "probability_yes": 0.38, "probability_no": 0.62, "probability_source": "independent", "confidence_yes": 0.42, "confidence_no": 0.58, "rationale": "BASE RATE: BO1 League of Legends matches are inherently high-variance. Single-game outcomes lack the stabilization of series play, making the base 50% prior reasonable before team-specific data. HEAD-TO-HEAD (WINTER 2026): Spandau vs BIG played 3+ times in Winter playoffs. Confirmed outcomes: Spandau won 1-0 once (Game 1 of series), but BIG won the series 3-1 overall and beat Spandau 3-0 in another Winter playoff match. Head-to-head record favors BIG 2-1 in decisive encounters. RECENT FORM ASYMMETRY: Briefing shows Spandau won 1-0 vs TOG and ROSS in Week 1-2 of Winter regular season, but these were against lower-ranked teams. Against BIG specifically (same tier), Spandau's BO1 record is 1-2 in Winter 2026. MACRO PERFORMANCE: In Winter playoffs, BIG demonstrated superior macro with 27 kills and 68.6% kill share in at least one match vs Spandau's 8 kills. This suggests BIG's team coordination and win conditions are cleaner. SPRING META RESET: This is Spring 2026 regular season (not Winter playoffs). Meta shifts and roster continuity details are sparse in briefing. Rosters show mid-season changes (dates 2025-11-12 through 2025-12-23), making recent form less predictive of current strength. Confidence reduced on both sides. MARKET PRICE DIVERGENCE: Market prices YES at 37% and NO at 63%. Consistent with head-to-head H2H favoring BIG (2-1) and BIG's playoff macro dominance, but BO1 variance means market may be somewhat overweighting historical performance. BO1 VOLATILITY: Single-game format means even a historically-weaker team has ~35-45% win probability against a stronger opponent (vs ~20-30% in BO3). Spandau's isolated BO1 wins suggest they are not helpless, but pattern shows BIG more reliable. TIMING: Event starts in ~3 hours (TODAY). No new roster information or meta announcements in briefing to shift expectations materially. Market has stabilized at 37/63; late-breaking information is unlikely. CONFIDENCE RATIONALE: confidence_yes = 42 (moderate) because Spandau has shown BO1 capability (1-0 vs BIG in Winter, multiple W vs lower teams), but Winter H2H and macro data favor BIG. confidence_no = 58 (moderate-high) because BIG's 2-1 H2H and superior macro play in playoffs is solid evidence, but BO1 variance and potential Spring meta shifts create uncertainty. Neither side has overwhelming evidence; asymmetry reflects BIG's stronger historical data.", "key_factor": "BO1 League of Legends matches in regional leagues show ~50% variance; recent head-to-head (Winter 2026) favors BIG 2-1 in playoffs; Spandau showed isolated BO1 wins in regular season but lost series convincingly"}, {"agent_name": "The Sentiment Analyst", "probability": 0.38, "probability_yes": 0.38, "probability_no": 0.62, "probability_source": "independent", "confidence_yes": 