 CALIBRATION: Both confidence scores are LOW (22 YES, 35 NO) because briefing provides zero tactical analysis. We are betting on market microstructure, not on team strength. The bull case for NO rests entirely on sustained directional repricing and the absence of counter-evidence. WHY NOT HIGHER NO CONFIDENCE: Academy teams are unpredictable; roster stability, individual player streaks, and map-specific matchups are invisible. A 73% favorite in BO3 can easily lose 1-2 if either team's meta exploits the opponent or a star player underperforms. Confidence on NO is capped at 35 because we have no data to rule out Phantom's upside. SUMMARY: NO (Spirit Academy) is favored by market repricing and marginal form, but the briefing is too thin to strongly defend either side. Probability_score of 28 (YES) / 68 (NO) reflects market consensus with appropriate uncertainty. The 4pp spread (28 + 68 = 96) reflects genuine ignorance mass on unmeasured academy team dynamics.", "key_factor": "Market consensus (73% NO) reflects Spirit Academy's structural form advantage (7W-3L vs 7W-2L), sustained by consistent upward price momentum over 7 days despite thin briefing data."}, {"agent_name": "The Bear", "probability": 0.32, "probability_yes": 0.32, "probability_no": 0.68, "probability_source": "independent", "confidence_yes": 0.28, "confidence_no": 0.35, "rationale": "FORM ADVANTAGE FOR YES: Phantom Academy shows 7W-2L over last 9 matches vs Spirit Academy's 7W-3L over 10 matches \u2014 Phantom has tighter win ratio (78% vs 70%) and sharper recent trajectory, contradicting the 27% YES pricing. MARKET PRICE DRIFT SIGNALS UNCERTAINTY: YES side has fallen 7.0pp in 24 hours and 7.0pp over 7 days; NO side simultaneously rose 7.5pp. This sharp, sustained drift coupled with low volume ($16 total) and high volatility (3.5pp/hr on YES) suggests thin liquidity driving prices rather than fundamental conviction. SHARP MOVE TIMING: A -7.5pp drop on YES at Apr 17 07:00 UTC (9.5 hours before match start) with no corresponding briefing intelligence suggests technical/liquidity-driven repricing, not information arrival. This is precisely when late traders or limit order cascades can distort academy-tier match odds. BO3 INHERENT VARIANCE: Best-of-3 formats in academy Counter-Strike carry higher upset variance than higher-tier play \u2014 map selection, momentum swings, and player familiarity matter disproportionately. Market may be applying higher-tier stability assumptions to academy play. DATA GAP ON BOTH SIDES: Briefing explicitly states 'no specific information about either team's recent form, roster composition, or head-to-head record' is available. This admission of ignorance should suppress confidence in both directions, yet market has compressed NO to 73%, suggesting false precision. INSUFFICIENT INTELLIGENCE FOR NO CONVICTION: The 73% NO price rests on zero concrete data about Spirit Academy's strengths, recent trajectory, or matchup edges. With only form ratios (7W-3L) and no context on opponent quality or meta alignment, the NO side's confidence should be capped. ACADEMY-TIER OPACITY: Exort Series Main Stage academy teams operate with minimal coverage. The briefing contains generic HLTV boilerplate and no player quotes, roster notes, or tournament-specific intel. This opacity should widen uncertainty bands, not narrow them to 27% vs 73%. UPSIDE CASE FOR YES: If Phantom's 78% win rate reflects genuine form momentum and map pool advantage, and Spirit's 70% hides losses to stronger opposition, YES at 27% captures upside. A 5-6pp swing (to 32-33%) is defensible given form parity and data scarcity. DOWNSIDE CASE FOR NO: Spirit Academy's 7W-3L and higher market confidence (73%) lacks supporting evidence in briefing. NO case rests entirely on price history and market sentiment, not fundamental analysis. Confidence in NO should be significantly lower than in YES. CONFIDENCE ASYMMETRY JUSTIFIED: YES confidence set to 28 (low, due to data gap) and NO confidence set to 35 (also low, but slightly higher to reflect market consensus weight). Neither side has strong data; the split reflects that YES has marginally better form evidence, while NO has only market momentum.", "key_factor": "Phantom Academy's superior recent form (7W-2L vs 7W-3L) and BO3 volatility in academy-tier CS suggest the market has over-weighted Spirit Academy based on insufficient data."}, {"agent_name": "The Base-Rate Statistician", "probability": 0.27, "probability_yes": 0.27, "probability_no": 0.73, "probability_source": "independent", "confidence_yes": 0.35, "confidence_no": 0.4, "rationale": "RECENT FORM ASYMMETRY: Phantom Academy 7W-2L (78% win rate, last 9) vs Spirit Academy 7W-3L (70% win rate, last 10). Phantom has marginally superior recent performance, contradicting 27% YES price. BASE RATE CONTEXT: Counter-Strike BO3 matches between academy-tier teams lack deep historical data. Typical underdog win rates in esports BO3s range 35-45%; Phantom Academy appears to be priced AS an underdog (27%) despite superior recent form. MARKET MISPRICING SIGNAL: YES price has fallen 7pp in 24h (now 27%), while NO rose 7.5pp (now 73%). Sharp move on Apr 17 06:00-07:00 UTC suggests information flow or sentiment shift, but briefing contains NO new competitive intelligence to justify the move. DATA GAPS CRITICAL: Briefing contains roster composition, head-to-head record, map pool analysis, or coaching staff intel \u2014 none available. Search results yield generic Counter-Strike news, not academy-specific metrics. This severely limits confidence on both sides. RECENCY PREMIUM: Phantom's 7W-2L record (last 9 matches) is more recent than Spirit's 7W-3L (last 10). In volatile esports scenes, recent form often outweighs older samples. This favors YES slightly but is weak evidence. LIVE/IMMINENT CAVEAT: Match starts in ~2.2 hours. Any reversal of market pricing now requires sudden roster changes, withdrawal announcements, or team statement \u2014 all low-probability in this window. Current 27/73 split likely reflects late-informed traders or model consensus. CONFIDENCE ASYMMETRY JUSTIFIED: confidence_yes=35 (weak evidence for Phantom despite better form; lack of detailed roster/map intel) and confidence_no=40 (Spirit is favored by market, but 7pp price move lacks corresponding briefing data). Both confidence scores reflect high ignorance mass. COMPARABLE BASE RATE: If academy BO3s follow esports norms, a team with +8% recent win rate (78% vs 70%) should command ~55-60% implied probability, not 27%. Market may be anchoring to older data, player roster changes, or unmeasured strength-of-schedule factors. RESOLUTION RISK MINIMAL: Match is in official tournament; forfeit/cancellation base rate <2% for scheduled Exort Series events. 50-50 tie resolution is negligible. FINAL ANCHOR: Maintain market price (27/73) as working estimate due to information asymmetry, but flag Phantom Academy as potentially UNDERPRICED relative to recent form. Confidence on both sides intentionally suppressed due to absent roster and map-specific data.", "key_factor": "Counter-Strike BO3 academy-tier matches show no strong directional bias; underdog win rate \u224835-45%. Market pricing (27% YES) aligns with or slightly underweights historical underdog rates."}, {"agent_name": "The Sentiment Analyst", "probability": 0.27, "probability_yes": 0.27, "probability_no": 0.73, "probability_source": "independent", "confidence_yes": 0.35, "confidence_no": 0.4, "rationale": "TIMING CRITICAL: Event is LIVE/IMMINENT (2.2 hours away). Any price move now reflects only final positioning, not new information discovery. Reversal risk is extremely low. PRICE ALIGNMENT: Current market price (YES 27% / NO 73%) reflects a moderate underdog-vs-favorite dynamic. This is rational given near-zero time to reversal. DATA VACUUM: Briefing contains NO actionable intelligence on either academy team's form, roster, preparation, or head-to-head history. The structured match data (7W-2L for Phantom Academy vs 7W-3L for Spirit Academy) contradicts the market price \u2014 Phantom Academy has BETTER recent form (7-2 vs 7-3) yet is priced as a 27% underdog. FORM-PRICE DISCONNECT: Phantom Academy's superior win rate (77.8% vs 70%) should push YES price higher than 27%, yet the crowd is betting against them. This suggests either: (a) crowd is overweighting Spirit Academy's perceived prestige/team strength, or (b) crowd has private information about team lineups/preparation that the briefing omits. INFORMATION ASYMMETRY: The briefing provides raw stats but zero context on roster changes, substitutions, meta preparation, or recent scrims. For LIVE/IMMINENT events, unpriced information becomes less relevant \u2014 the market has already processed what it will process. SENTIMENT WEAKNESS ON BOTH SIDES: No emotional intensity, no crowd exuberance, no panic. The briefing is almost purely mechanical. This suggests RATIONAL PRICING driven by quiet hand-off between informed traders, not crowd psychology. CROWD IS NOT WRONG: A 27/73 split for an academy team match with minimal media coverage is reasonable. The crowd is likely anchoring on Spirit Academy's perceived tier/pedigree, not hysteria. CONFIDENCE ASYMMETRY: YES-side confidence is LOW (35) because the briefing provides no narrative to justify backing Phantom Academy despite their superior form. NO-side confidence is also LOW (40) because the briefing provides no narrative to justify the 73% price either \u2014 we are flying blind. The sum is intentionally <100 to reflect genuine ignorance. MARKET WILL NOT REVERSE: With 2.2 hours to match start, a reversal would require a massive news shock (roster announcement, threat, injury). This is improbable in the next 2 hours for an academy-level CS match. VERDICT: Market price is COHERENT but UNDER-INFORMED. The current 27/73 split is rational given uncertainty, but it diverges slightly from the win-rate data (which would suggest closer to 35/65). This is not a crowd psychology mispricing \u2014 it is rational apathy toward academy-level CS combined with informational disadvantage.", "key_factor": "Apathy"}, {"agent_name": "The Domain Specialist", "probability": 0.28, "probability_yes": 0.28, "probability_no": 0.7, "probability_source": "independent", "confidence_yes": 0.32, "confidence_no": 0.35, "rationale": "RECORD COMPARISON: Phantom Academy 7W-2L (78% win rate, 9 matches); Spirit Academy 7W-3L (70% win rate, 10 matches). Raw win rates favor Phantom by ~8pp, but sample sizes are small and overlapping periods unclear. PRICE DYNAMICS: YES (Phantom) has fallen 7pp over 24h to 27%; NO (Spirit) has risen 7.5pp to 73%. Sharp move at Apr 17 07:00 UTC (-7.5pp YES) suggests information influx or market repricing. This timing (13 hours pre-match) is unusual and may reflect lineup changes, scrimmage results, or coach/analyst commentary not captured in briefing. ACADEMY-TIER VOLATILITY: Counter-Strike academy competitions have high roster churn, frequent stand-ins, and inconsistent preparation levels. 'Academy' designation implies developmental rosters with lower institutional stability than tier-1 orgs. Match outcomes often hinge on single-map performance quirks rather than sustained team cohesion. MARKET STRUCTURE CONCERN: Only $16 USD volume indicates extremely thin liquidity. Price movements of 7-7.5pp on low volume suggest potential manipulation or minority trader consensus rather than market-wide confidence. Resolution will occur within 6 hours (BO3 typical duration ~2-3 hours), leaving minimal reversal time. MISSING CRITICAL DATA: Briefing lacks roster composition, map pool matchups, recent scrim results, player substitutions, or opponent-specific preparation. In academy esports, these factors are often more predictive than historical W-L records due to roster volatility. HEAD-TO-HEAD HISTORY: No prior encounter mentioned between Phantom Academy and Spirit Academy in briefing. Cannot calibrate performance differential from direct precedent. EXORT SERIES CONTEXT: Exort Series is a secondary/regional circuit. Teams competing here often juggle primary roster priorities, leading to deprioritized match preparation and substitution patterns that undermine predictability. MARKET ASYMMETRY: Confidence_no set to 35 (not higher) because the case AGAINST Phantom is similarly thin\u2014Spirit's 70% win rate is not substantially better, and the 7pp price movement may reflect noise rather than fundamental team strength. Symmetrical data poverty argues for near-parity confidence on both sides.", "key_factor": "Academy-tier CS:GO/CS2 matches are characterized by extreme volatility and thin information asymmetry; the market's 73% NO price likely reflects Spirit Academy's marginally superior recent record (7W-3L vs 7W-2L over different sample sizes) and momentum shift, but academy rosters are highly unstable and prone to upsets."}, {"agent_name": "The Contrarian", "probability": 0.31, "probability_yes": 0.31, "probability_no": 0.69, "probability_source": "independent", "confidence_yes": 0.28, "confidence_no": 0.32, "rationale": "LIVE/IMMINENT STATUS: Event starts in ~2.2 hours. The market price of 27% YES reflects extreme confidence in Spirit Academy, but at this stage, any roster change, illness, or tactical surprise would have minimal time for correction before lock. This creates high asymmetry in black-swan leverage. CRITICAL DATA GAP: Briefing contains ZERO substantive information on either team's recent roster, form trajectory, map pool, or head-to-head record. The 'NEWS SUMMARY' explicitly states 'No direct intelligence on either academy team's current competitive status or preparation.' This is highly unusual for a scheduled match 2.2 hours away. PHANTOM ACADEMY'S HIDDEN ANGLE: Recent form shows 7W-2L (78% win rate, last 9 matches) vs Spirit Academy's 7W-3L (70% win rate, last 10 matches). The headline suggests Spirit is favored, yet Phantom has a SUPERIOR form trajectory. Market is pricing this as 73% NO, which appears to OVERWEIGHT Spirit despite equal or slightly inferior recent performance. ACADEMY-LEVEL VOLATILITY: Both teams are academy franchises in a lower-tier tournament (Exort Series Main Stage). Academy teams exhibit higher tactical unpredictability, weaker scouting, and less refined anti-strats than tier-1 rosters. A single well-executed map pick or unprepared opponent can swing BO3 decisively. SHARP MOVE SIGNAL: Market moved -7.5pp sharply on Apr 17 06:00 UTC (Spirit favored further), followed by another -7.0pp drop 07:00 UTC. These moves correlate with information release (likely social media, Discord, or team announcements), but the briefing does not capture WHAT information triggered the move. Possibility: this information is outdated, misinterpreted, or reflects Spirit hype rather than fundamental edge. LOW VOLUME / THIN MARKET: Only $16 USD total volume suggests extremely light positioning. No large professional bookmakers or syndicates are actively trading. This micro-liquidity market is highly vulnerable to sentiment swings and may not reflect true competitive odds. BLACK SWAN SCENARIO: Phantom Academy announces a roster substitution (e.g., stand-in IGL, substitute rifler) 90 minutes before match start, exploiting Spirit's lack of preparation. Alternatively, Phantom's coach deploys a non-standard map veto (e.g., banning Inferno instead of expected pick, forcing Spirit into unfamiliar territory) that cracks Spirit's academy-level adaptability. Neither of these events is implausible for an academy match; both have precedent in tier-2/3 CS. CONFIDENCE RATIONALE: confidence_yes=28 and confidence_no=32 reflect near-total asymmetric uncertainty. The briefing provides ZERO directional evidence on either team's actual strength, meta-fit, or readiness. Phantom's superior raw form (78% vs 70%) is contradicted by market confidence in Spirit. This inversion itself is a red flag\u2014either the market knows something unreported, or it is overcorrecting to noise. Given the lack of corroborating data, both confidence scores remain well below 50. OUTCOME PATH FOR YES (LOW-PROBABILITY, HIGH-IMPACT): Phantom deploys unprepared meta, Spirit's academy roster fails to adapt in live BO3 setting, Phantom takes map 1, momentum shifts, Phantom wins 2-0 or 2-1. Probability ~25-35% after accounting for form diff and tactical edge. OUTCOME PATH FOR NO (STRONGER BUT NOT CERTAIN): Spirit Academy is favored for reasons the market has priced but the briefing has not disclosed (e.g., recent scrim results, roster stability, coach reputation). Market conviction at 73% reflects this hidden information advantage. However, thin liquidity and academy-level volatility prevent confidence from exceeding 35-40%.", "key_factor": "Phantom Academy deploys an unconventional map veto or tactical pivot that exploits Spirit Academy's academy-level predictability, combined with a roster substitution or last-minute lineup adjustment that the market has not priced in."}], "final_score": 0.28324107452820324, "no_score": 0.7164327764745974, "edge": 0.4209090909090909, "no_edge": -0.039090909090909065, "recommendation": "PASS", "confidence_level": "LOW", "signal_type": null, "pass_reason": "NO-side confidence too low: 36.00% (need \u226567%)", "expected_roi": 0.0, "perceived_win_rate_at_run": 0.6909090909090909, "kelly_stake_full": 0.0, "kelly_stake_scaled": 0.0, "kelly_multiplier": 0.0, "agent_weights_snapshot": {"The Rules Lawyer": 1.0508778629618616, "The Bull": 0.9407098700352481, "The Base-Rate Statistician": 1.0540798334052115, "The Sentiment Analyst": 1.0537396973507898, "The Domain Specialist": 1.0553403376068922, "The Contrarian": 1.0735033923024546, "The Bear": 0.7717490063375423}, "briefing": {"market": {"id": "2003495", "question": "Counter-Strike:  Phantom Academy vs Spirit Academy (BO3) - Exort Series Main Stage", "resolution_criteria": "This market refers to the Counter-Strike Round 2 match between  Phantom Academy and Spirit Academy in the Exort Series Main Stage, initially scheduled for April 17 at 2:30PM ET.\n\nThis market will resolve to \" Phantom Academy\" if  Phantom Academy win the match against Spirit Academy.\n\nThis market will resolve to \"Spirit Academy\" if Spirit Academy win the match against  Phantom Academy.\n\nIf the match is canceled (not played at all), ends in a tie, or is delayed beyond 7 days from the scheduled date without a winner determined, this market will resolve to 50-50. \n\nIf the match begins but is not completed, and one team wins due to the opponent's forfeiture, disqualification, or walkover, this market will resolve to the team who wins. \n\nIf the match ends in a forfeit, disqualification, or walkover (team withdraws before the start and the other wins automatically), this market will resolve to 50-50. \n\nThe resolution source for this market will be official information from https://hltv.org. However, if https://hltv.org has not published final results within 2 hours after the event\u2019s conclusion, a consensus of credible reporting may be used instead including video evidence.\n\nIn cases where a team\u2019s listed name includes minor discrepancies from the resolution source, this market will resolve based on the underlying real-world match rather than exact name matching. Recognizable abbreviations, alternate or erroneous spellings, sponsor tags, affiliate or academy designations, regional identifiers, and minor formatting differences will be treated as referring to the same team, provided the intended team can be clearly and uniquely identified within the relevant competition. If a listed team name has no reasonable connection to any participating team, or if it matches or could reasonably refer to another team in the same competition such that the intended team cannot be unambiguously determined, this market will resolve 50-50.", "end_date": "2026-04-18T00:30:00Z", "event_start_time": "2026-04-17T18:30:00Z", "yes_price": 0.27, "volume_usd": 15.641181, "category": "co